var e=[{topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Every measurement in the laboratory carries some uncertainty. Pharmaceutical analysis begins by naming that uncertainty, deciding how much of a reading you are entitled to write down, and separating mistakes you can correct from scatter you can only average out.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Words you must define first`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Accuracy: how close a result is to the true or accepted value.`,`Precision: how close repeated results are to each other, regardless of whether they are right.`,`Error: measured value minus true value. It has a sign.`,`Deviation: a single result minus the mean of the set. It measures precision, not accuracy.`,`Bias: a systematic shift of all results in one direction.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Precise is not accurate`,text:`A burette with a wrongly etched scale gives four readings that agree to 0.01 mL and are all wrong by 0.5 mL. That is high precision with poor accuracy. Good accuracy without precision is possible too, when large random errors happen to cancel.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two kinds of error`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Determinate (systematic)`,`Indeterminate (random)`],rows:[[`Cause`,`Traceable and assignable`,`Unknown, many small causes`],[`Sign`,`Same direction every time`,`Positive and negative equally`],[`Effect`,`Spoils accuracy`,`Spoils precision`],[`Detected by`,`Standard sample, blank, other method`,`Spread of replicates`],[`Removed by`,`Calibration and correction`,`Cannot remove; average it out`]],caption:`The determinate versus indeterminate split is the single most asked idea in this topic.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Determinate errors are sub-classified as instrumental (uncalibrated weights, worn burette tip), method (co-precipitation, incomplete reaction, side reaction) and personal (parallax, colour blindness at an end point).`,`Method errors are the hardest to detect because repeating the analysis reproduces them faithfully.`,`Indeterminate error follows a normal (Gaussian) distribution, so its mean tends to zero as replicate number rises.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Absolute and relative error`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Absolute error E = xi - xt   |   Relative error = (xi - xt) / xt x 100 percent`,caption:`xi = measured value, xt = true value. Relative error is often quoted in parts per thousand (ppt) by multiplying by 1000 instead of 100.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Constant versus proportional`,text:`A constant determinate error (say 0.5 mg lost to solubility in a wash) hurts a small sample far more than a large one, so its relative error falls as sample size rises. A proportional error keeps the same relative size at every sample weight.`}]},{topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must count significant figures correctly through a calculation, compute standard deviation and coefficient of variation, and apply the rules for propagating error through addition and multiplication.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Significant figures`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`All non-zero digits are significant.`,`Zeros between non-zero digits are significant: 1.007 has four.`,`Leading zeros are never significant: 0.00250 has three (2, 5 and the trailing zero).`,`Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant: 2.50 has three.`,`Trailing zeros in a whole number are ambiguous; 2500 is written 2.5 x 10^3 to show two.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Addition and subtraction: the answer keeps the fewest decimal places of any term.`,`Multiplication and division: the answer keeps the fewest significant figures of any factor.`,`Logarithms: the mantissa carries the significant figures. log(2.0 x 10^-3) = -2.70, two figures after the point because 2.0 has two significant figures.`,`Exact numbers (a count of 5 tablets, the 1000 in mg to g) have infinite significant figures and never limit the answer.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Round only at the end`,text:`Carry one extra digit through intermediate steps and round once at the final answer. Rounding at every step accumulates a determinate rounding bias. Use the round-to-even rule for a trailing 5: 2.35 becomes 2.4 but 2.45 becomes 2.4.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Measures of precision`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`s = sqrt( sum(xi - xbar)^2 / (n - 1) )`,caption:`Sample standard deviation. xi = each result, xbar = mean, n = number of replicates, (n-1) = degrees of freedom.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`RSD or CV = (s / xbar) x 100 percent`,caption:`Relative standard deviation, also called coefficient of variation. Dimensionless, so it lets you compare precision across methods and units.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Standard error of mean SEM = s / sqrt(n)`,caption:`s = sample SD, n = number of replicates. SEM shrinks with the square root of n, which is why quadrupling replicates only halves the uncertainty of the mean.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Propagation of error`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Operation`,`Combine`,`Rule`],rows:[[`y = a + b - c`,`Absolute SDs`,`sy = sqrt(sa^2 + sb^2 + sc^2)`],[`y = a x b / c`,`Relative SDs`,`sy/y = sqrt((sa/a)^2 + (sb/b)^2 + (sc/c)^2)`],[`y = log a`,`Relative to absolute`,`sy = 0.434 x (sa/a)`],[`y = a^x`,`Relative x power`,`sy/y = x (sa/a)`]],caption:`Add absolute uncertainties in quadrature for sums, relative uncertainties in quadrature for products.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Weighing by difference`,text:`Two weighings each with SD 0.1 mg give a net weight with SD sqrt(0.1^2 + 0.1^2) = 0.14 mg, not 0.2 mg. The quadrature rule is a favourite one-line numerical.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Range (w) = highest minus lowest value; quick but wasteful of information.`,`Mean deviation = sum of |xi - xbar| / n; largely historical, replaced by SD.`,`Variance = s^2; it is variances, not SDs, that are additive.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions ask you to reject an outlier defensibly, quote a confidence interval, and read the validation vocabulary of ICH Q2 which frames accuracy and precision in regulatory language.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Confidence interval`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`CI = xbar +/- (t x s) / sqrt(n)`,caption:`t = Student t value for (n-1) degrees of freedom at the chosen confidence level, s = sample SD, n = replicates. Use z instead of t only when sigma is known.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The t value shrinks towards 1.96 (the 95 percent z value) as n grows large.`,`A wider confidence level (99 percent) gives a wider interval for the same data.`,`Confidence interval describes the mean; a tolerance interval describes individual future results.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Rejecting a suspect result`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = |suspect value - nearest neighbour| / range`,caption:`Dixon Q test. Reject the suspect value if calculated Q exceeds the tabulated Q at the stated confidence for that n.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Grubbs test uses G = |suspect - xbar| / s and is the test recommended by ISO and IUPAC over Dixon Q.`,`Never discard a value merely because it looks wrong; a documented assignable cause or a passed statistical test is required.`,`If a result is rejected, report how many results were rejected out of how many measured.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`ICH Q2 validation vocabulary`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`What it measures`,`Usual expression`],rows:[[`Accuracy`,`Closeness to true value`,`Percent recovery, 98-102 percent`],[`Repeatability`,`Same analyst, same day`,`RSD of 6 determinations`],[`Intermediate precision`,`Different day, analyst, equipment`,`RSD across conditions`],[`Reproducibility`,`Different laboratories`,`Collaborative trial RSD`],[`Specificity`,`Response to analyte alone`,`Peak purity, resolution`],[`LOD`,`Lowest detectable amount`,`3.3 x sigma / slope`],[`LOQ`,`Lowest quantifiable amount`,`10 x sigma / slope`]],caption:`sigma = SD of the response (blank or intercept), slope = slope of the calibration line.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`LOD and LOQ ratios`,text:`The signal-to-noise convention is 3:1 for LOD and 10:1 for LOQ; the calibration-curve convention is 3.3 sigma/S and 10 sigma/S. LOQ is roughly three times LOD. Both forms are examined.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`RAP for random error`,text:`Random errors are Reduced by Averaging and are Positive-and-negative. Determinate errors are Directional and Detectable by a Different method.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Comparing two means uses the Student t test; comparing two precisions uses the F test (F = s1^2/s2^2 with the larger variance on top).`,`A method free of determinate error should recover a certified reference material within its stated uncertainty.`,`Youden plot and standard addition are the classical tools for exposing proportional determinate error in a matrix.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`An acid-base titration finds how much acid or base a sample contains by adding a solution of exactly known strength until the reaction is complete. Everything else in this topic exists to answer two questions: when is it complete, and how do you know.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The vocabulary`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Titrant: the solution of known concentration in the burette.`,`Analyte or titrand: the substance being measured, in the flask.`,`Standard solution: a titrant whose concentration is accurately known.`,`Standardisation: finding the exact concentration of a titrant against a primary standard.`,`Equivalence point: the theoretical point where stoichiometrically equal amounts have reacted.`,`End point: the point you actually observe, usually a colour change.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Equivalence point versus end point`,text:`The equivalence point is a calculated fact; the end point is an experimental observation. The gap between them is the titration error. A good indicator makes that gap negligible, it never abolishes it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Primary and secondary standards`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Requirement`,`Primary standard`,`Secondary standard`],rows:[[`Purity`,`Very high, 99.9 percent plus`,`Ordinary reagent grade`],[`Stability`,`Not hygroscopic, not oxidised`,`May absorb water or CO2`],[`Use`,`Weighed directly to make a solution`,`Standardised against a primary`],[`Equivalent weight`,`High, so weighing error is small`,`Not a criterion`],[`Examples`,`Sodium carbonate, potassium hydrogen phthalate, oxalic acid`,`NaOH, HCl, KMnO4, sodium thiosulphate`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Sodium hydroxide cannot be a primary standard because it is deliquescent and absorbs atmospheric carbon dioxide.`,`Hydrochloric acid cannot be a primary standard because concentrated HCl loses hydrogen chloride gas; only constant boiling HCl is used that way.`,`Potassium hydrogen phthalate (KHP, equivalent weight 204.2) is the standard of choice for NaOH.`,`Anhydrous sodium carbonate (equivalent weight 53.0) is the standard of choice for HCl.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The working equation`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`N1 V1 = N2 V2`,caption:`N = normality, V = volume in mL. Valid only when both are expressed as normality (equivalents), because normality already carries the stoichiometry.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Normality = Molarity x n-factor   |   Equivalent weight = Molecular weight / n-factor`,caption:`n-factor is the number of replaceable H+ for an acid, replaceable OH- for a base, or electrons exchanged in a redox reaction.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Read the definition of an indicator`,text:`An acid-base indicator is itself a weak acid or weak base whose ionised and unionised forms have different colours. That definition, not the colour list, is what most one-mark questions target.`}]},{topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level of this topic is the titration curve: knowing the pH at the equivalence point for each of the four acid-base combinations, and picking an indicator whose transition range falls inside the steep part of that curve.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Indicator theory`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`pH = pKIn + log([In-]/[HIn])`,caption:`Henderson-Hasselbalch for an indicator. pKIn = indicator dissociation exponent, HIn = acid colour form, In- = base colour form.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The eye sees one colour when the ratio is 10:1 and the other at 1:10, so the visible range is pKIn +/- 1, about two pH units wide.`,`Ostwald theory explains indicator behaviour by ionisation; the quinonoid theory explains it by tautomeric change of structure. Both are examined.`,`A mixed indicator (bromocresol green plus methyl red) narrows the transition to a sharper colour change.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Indicator`,`pH range`,`Acid colour`,`Alkaline colour`],rows:[[`Thymol blue (1st)`,`1.2 - 2.8`,`Red`,`Yellow`],[`Methyl orange`,`3.1 - 4.4`,`Red`,`Yellow`],[`Bromocresol green`,`3.8 - 5.4`,`Yellow`,`Blue`],[`Methyl red`,`4.2 - 6.3`,`Red`,`Yellow`],[`Bromothymol blue`,`6.0 - 7.6`,`Yellow`,`Blue`],[`Phenol red`,`6.8 - 8.4`,`Yellow`,`Red`],[`Phenolphthalein`,`8.3 - 10.0`,`Colourless`,`Pink`],[`Thymolphthalein`,`9.3 - 10.5`,`Colourless`,`Blue`]],caption:`Learn methyl orange and phenolphthalein exactly; the rest are recognised by band position.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four titration curves`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`System`,`pH at equivalence`,`Steep range`,`Indicator`],rows:[[`Strong acid - strong base`,`7.0`,`4 to 10`,`Any: methyl orange or phenolphthalein`],[`Weak acid - strong base`,`Above 7 (about 8.7)`,`7 to 10`,`Phenolphthalein`],[`Strong acid - weak base`,`Below 7 (about 5.3)`,`4 to 6.5`,`Methyl orange or methyl red`],[`Weak acid - weak base`,`Near 7, no steep jump`,`Almost none`,`No visual indicator; use potentiometry`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`PWA and MOWB`,text:`Phenolphthalein for Weak Acid, Methyl Orange for Weak Base. The indicator goes with the weak partner because the salt hydrolyses towards that partner.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Points on the curve worth knowing`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`At half-neutralisation of a weak acid, pH = pKa`,caption:`Half the acid is converted to salt, so [salt] = [acid] and the log term vanishes. This is the standard way of measuring pKa by titration.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`pH at equivalence (weak acid + strong base) = 7 + 0.5 pKa + 0.5 log C`,caption:`C = concentration of the salt formed. The salt of a weak acid hydrolyses to give an alkaline solution.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A weak acid must have Ka greater than about 10^-8 to be titrated in water with a visible end point.`,`Polyprotic acids give one inflection per proton if successive Ka values differ by a factor of about 10^4.`,`Phosphoric acid shows two usable end points (methyl orange for the first, phenolphthalein for the second); the third is too weak to see.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Double indicator method`,text:`For a carbonate-hydroxide or carbonate-bicarbonate mixture, phenolphthalein volume (P) covers all the hydroxide plus half the carbonate; the further methyl orange volume (M) covers the rest. If P > M there is NaOH + Na2CO3; if P < M there is Na2CO3 + NaHCO3; if P = M it is pure carbonate.`}]},{topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work covers back titration arithmetic, the Kjeldahl determination, buffer capacity, and the quantitative limits on what water will let you titrate.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Back (residual) titration`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Add a known excess of standard reagent to the sample and let it react completely.`,`Titrate the unreacted excess with a second standard solution.`,`Equivalents consumed by sample = equivalents added - equivalents found in the back titration.`,`Run a blank identically without sample; the difference between blank and sample titre gives the sample directly.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Back titration is used when the reaction is slow (magnesium oxide with HCl), when the sample is insoluble (calcium carbonate), or when no suitable direct end point exists (aspirin hydrolysis with excess NaOH).`,`Aspirin assay: excess NaOH hydrolyses acetylsalicylic acid to salicylate plus acetate, and the surplus alkali is titrated with sulphuric acid.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Kjeldahl nitrogen`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Digest with concentrated H2SO4, potassium sulphate to raise the boiling point and copper or selenium catalyst; organic nitrogen becomes ammonium sulphate.`,`Make alkaline with excess NaOH and distil the liberated ammonia into a receiver.`,`Receive into boric acid (then titrate the borate with standard acid) or into a known excess of standard acid (then back titrate with alkali).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Boric acid receiver needs no standardisation`,text:`Boric acid is too weak to be titrated itself, so an inexact excess is fine; only the ammonium borate formed is titrated with standard acid using methyl red. That is why the boric acid modification is preferred.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Buffers`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Buffer capacity beta = dB / d(pH)   |   Maximum when pH = pKa`,caption:`dB = gram equivalents of strong base or acid added per litre, d(pH) = resulting pH change. Useful buffer range is pKa +/- 1.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Van Slyke: beta = 2.303 C Ka [H3O+] / (Ka + [H3O+])^2`,caption:`C = total buffer concentration. At pH = pKa this reduces to beta = 0.576 C.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Concept`,`Definition`,`Note`],rows:[[`Titration error`,`End point volume - equivalence volume`,`Corrected by blank`],[`Blank titration`,`Same procedure without sample`,`Removes reagent and indicator error`],[`Sharpness index`,`d(pH)/dV at equivalence`,`Falls as acid gets weaker`],[`Levelling effect`,`All strong acids equally strong in water`,`Water levels anything stronger than H3O+`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why the curve flattens`,text:`The steep jump narrows as the acid gets weaker AND as the solution gets more dilute. Both a low Ka and a low concentration can destroy the end point, which is exactly why non-aqueous titration exists.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carbon dioxide dissolved in NaOH creates carbonate error; titrating to a phenolphthalein end point counts only half the carbonate, so carbonate-free alkali is prepared by using saturated NaOH and decanting.`,`A potentiometric end point (steepest dE/dV, or the second derivative crossing zero) removes all indicator error and is used for coloured or turbid samples.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A redox titration measures an analyte by transferring electrons instead of protons. One partner is oxidised, the other reduced, and the end point is the moment the electron transfer is complete.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two half-processes`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Oxidation: loss of electrons, increase in oxidation number.`,`Reduction: gain of electrons, decrease in oxidation number.`,`Oxidising agent: accepts electrons and is itself reduced (KMnO4, K2Cr2O7, Ce4+, I2).`,`Reducing agent: donates electrons and is itself oxidised (sodium thiosulphate, oxalic acid, ferrous ammonium sulphate, ascorbic acid).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`OIL RIG and LEO the lion says GER`,text:`Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons. Loss of Electrons is Oxidation, Gain of Electrons is Reduction.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four main systems`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Titrant`,`Medium`,`End point`],rows:[[`Permanganometry`,`KMnO4`,`Dilute H2SO4`,`Self-indicating pink`],[`Dichrometry`,`K2Cr2O7`,`Acid`,`Diphenylamine, violet`],[`Cerimetry`,`Ce(SO4)2`,`Acid`,`Ferroin, red to pale blue`],[`Iodine methods`,`I2 or Na2S2O3`,`Neutral to weak acid`,`Starch, blue`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`n-factor for KMnO4 in acid medium is 5, so equivalent weight = 158 / 5 = 31.6.`,`n-factor for K2Cr2O7 is 6, so equivalent weight = 294.2 / 6 = 49.03.`,`n-factor for iodine (I2 to 2I-) is 2; for sodium thiosulphate it is 1.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`KMnO4 is self-indicating`,text:`One drop of excess permanganate colours the solution pale pink, so no external indicator is needed. This makes permanganometry the classic example of a self-indicating titration.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Starch as indicator`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Starch forms an intense blue adsorption complex with iodine in the presence of iodide.`,`Add starch only near the end point in iodometry, otherwise iodine is trapped irreversibly in the starch and the end point drags.`,`Starch fails in strongly acid or hot solution and in high alcohol content.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the iodimetry versus iodometry distinction, the conditions each titrant demands, and the equivalent weights used in the assay calculation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Iodimetry versus iodometry`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Iodimetry (direct)`,`Iodometry (indirect)`],rows:[[`Titrant`,`Standard iodine solution`,`Standard sodium thiosulphate`],[`Analyte`,`A reducing agent`,`An oxidising agent`],[`Iodine role`,`Iodine is the oxidant added`,`Iodine is liberated then titrated`],[`Key step`,`Analyte + I2 directly`,`Analyte + excess KI gives I2`],[`Examples`,`Ascorbic acid, sodium thiosulphate, As2O3, sulphite`,`Copper sulphate, bleaching powder, chlorine, peroxide`],[`End point`,`Colourless to blue`,`Blue to colourless`]],caption:`Direct means the iodine is in the burette; indirect means the iodine is generated in the flask.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which way does the blue go`,text:`Iodimetry ends colourless to blue because the first excess of titrant iodine hits the starch. Iodometry ends blue to colourless because thiosulphate consumes the liberated iodine. Getting this backwards is the single commonest error in the topic.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`I2 + 2 Na2S2O3 -> 2 NaI + Na2S4O6`,caption:`Thiosulphate is oxidised to tetrathionate. One mole of iodine consumes two moles of thiosulphate, so the thiosulphate n-factor is 1.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Permanganometry in detail`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`MnO4- + 8 H+ + 5 e- -> Mn2+ + 4 H2O   (E0 = +1.51 V)`,caption:`Acid medium, five-electron change. In neutral or alkaline medium permanganate goes only to MnO2, a three-electron change (equivalent weight 158/3 = 52.7).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Only dilute sulphuric acid is used. Hydrochloric acid is oxidised to chlorine (an induced reaction) and nitric acid is itself an oxidant.`,`Titration of oxalic acid is done hot, at 60 to 70 degrees C, because the reaction is slow at room temperature.`,`It is autocatalytic: the Mn2+ produced catalyses the further reaction, so the first drop decolourises slowly and later drops fast.`,`Standard KMnO4 is not a primary standard; it is standardised against sodium oxalate, oxalic acid or arsenic trioxide.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cerimetry and dichrometry`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Point`,`Cerium(IV) sulphate`,`Potassium dichromate`],rows:[[`E0`,`+1.44 V in H2SO4`,`+1.33 V`],[`Electron change`,`1 (Ce4+ to Ce3+)`,`6 (Cr2O7 2- to 2 Cr3+)`],[`Primary standard`,`Ceric ammonium sulphate can be`,`Yes, K2Cr2O7 is a true primary standard`],[`Indicator`,`Ferroin or N-phenylanthranilic acid`,`Diphenylamine sulphonate`],[`Stability`,`Stable in acid, no interference from Cl-`,`Very stable, usable with HCl`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Cerimetry advantages`,text:`Ce(IV) solutions are stable indefinitely, can be boiled, are usable in hydrochloric acid, and involve a simple one-electron change with no intermediate oxidation states, so no side products form. These four advantages are the standard answer.`}]},{topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced redox work is the Nernst equation, the position of the equivalence point on a redox curve, and the specialist reagents such as Karl Fischer and bromatometry.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E = E0 - (0.0592/n) log([reduced]/[oxidised])   at 25 degrees C`,caption:`E = observed potential, E0 = standard potential, n = electrons transferred. The Nernst equation gives the shape of the redox titration curve.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E at equivalence = (n1 E1 + n2 E2) / (n1 + n2)`,caption:`Weighted mean of the two standard potentials when no H+ appears in either half reaction. n1, n2 = electron changes of the two couples.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A redox indicator changes colour when the potential passes its own E0; the transition range is E0 +/- 0.0592/n volts.`,`Ferroin (tris-phenanthroline iron(II)) has E0 = +1.06 V and is the standard indicator for cerimetry.`,`Diphenylamine (E0 = +0.76 V) needs phosphoric acid to be added in dichrometry of iron, to lower the Fe3+/Fe2+ potential by complexing Fe3+ and sharpen the end point.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Karl Fischer titration`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`I2 + SO2 + 2 H2O -> 2 HI + H2SO4`,caption:`The water-consuming reaction. The classical reagent is iodine, sulphur dioxide, pyridine and methanol; modern reagents replace pyridine with imidazole.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Karl Fischer is the pharmacopoeial method for water content; the end point is detected by dead-stop (biamperometric) electrometry.`,`One mole of iodine reacts with one mole of water; methanol is essential as it forms the intermediate methyl sulphite ester.`,`Coulometric KF generates iodine electrically and suits trace water (10 microgram to 200 mg); volumetric KF suits higher water content.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Other redox systems`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`System`,`Reagent`,`Typical assay`],rows:[[`Bromatometry`,`KBrO3 + KBr in acid`,`Phenol, isoniazid, sulphonamides`],[`Iodatometry`,`KIO3 in strong HCl`,`Andrews titration, iodine to ICl`],[`Titanous chloride`,`TiCl3, strong reductant`,`Nitro and azo compounds`],[`Nitrite titration`,`NaNO2 in HCl at 0-5 C`,`Primary aromatic amines, diazotisation`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Diazotisation details`,text:`Sodium nitrite titration of a primary aromatic amine (sulphanilamide, procaine) is done in hydrochloric acid below 5 degrees C with potassium bromide as catalyst; the end point is starch-iodide paper turning blue, or an electrometric dead-stop end point.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Thiosulphate is unstable`,text:`Sodium thiosulphate solution decomposes by acid, by carbon dioxide and by Thiobacillus bacteria, so it is prepared with freshly boiled cooled water, a little sodium carbonate, and restandardised against potassium dichromate or potassium iodate periodically.`}]},{topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Two families of titration share this topic. Complexometry measures metal ions by wrapping them in a complexing agent; precipitation titrimetry measures ions by making them fall out of solution as an insoluble salt.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Complexometry vocabulary`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ligand: a molecule or ion that donates a lone pair to a metal ion.`,`Chelate: a ring complex formed when one ligand binds through two or more donor atoms.`,`Denticity: the number of donor atoms one ligand uses. EDTA is hexadentate.`,`Coordination number: total donor atoms bound to the metal, commonly 4 or 6.`,`Stability constant Kf: the equilibrium constant for metal plus ligand giving complex. Bigger Kf means a sharper end point.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`EDTA is hexadentate and reacts 1:1`,text:`Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid offers two nitrogen and four carboxylate oxygen donors, so it forms a 1:1 complex with almost every metal ion whatever the metal charge. The 1:1 stoichiometry is what makes the calculation easy and is asked constantly.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The free acid of EDTA is poorly soluble, so the disodium salt (disodium edetate, Na2H2Y.2H2O, MW 372.24) is used.`,`Each mole of complex formed releases two moles of H+, so the solution must be buffered.`,`The usual buffer is ammonia and ammonium chloride at pH 10 for calcium and magnesium.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Metal ion indicators`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Indicator`,`pH used`,`Free colour`,`Metal complex colour`],rows:[[`Eriochrome black T`,`10`,`Blue`,`Wine red`],[`Murexide`,`12`,`Violet`,`Red or yellow`],[`Calcon (Patton-Reeder)`,`12 - 13`,`Blue`,`Pink`],[`Xylenol orange`,`5 - 6`,`Yellow`,`Red`],[`Pyrocatechol violet`,`2 - 6`,`Yellow`,`Blue`]],caption:`A metal ion indicator is itself a weaker chelating agent; EDTA strips the metal from it at the end point.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which colour is the end point`,text:`With eriochrome black T the solution is wine red while metal is present and turns blue at the end point when EDTA has taken the last metal ion. Students frequently quote the reverse.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Precipitation titrations at a glance`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Argentometry means titration with silver nitrate, used for chloride, bromide, iodide and thiocyanate.`,`The three classical methods are Mohr, Volhard and Fajans.`,`Silver nitrate solution is standardised against pure sodium chloride, which is a primary standard.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions here turn on two comparison tables: the four ways of doing an EDTA titration with masking chemistry, and the three argentometric methods with their indicators and pH constraints.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Types of EDTA titration`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`How it works`,`Example`],rows:[[`Direct`,`Titrate metal with EDTA`,`Calcium gluconate, magnesium sulphate`],[`Back`,`Add excess EDTA, titrate surplus with Zn or Mg`,`Aluminium, bismuth, lead`],[`Replacement`,`Metal displaces Mg from Mg-EDTA, titrate freed Mg`,`Calcium with EBT`],[`Indirect (alkalimetric)`,`Titrate the 2 H+ released with NaOH`,`Sulphate as barium, phosphate`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Back titration is used when the metal reacts slowly with EDTA, precipitates at the buffer pH, or blocks the indicator.`,`Indicator blocking means the metal binds the indicator so tightly that EDTA cannot free it; copper, cobalt, nickel and aluminium block EBT.`,`Blocking is cured by masking the offending ion or by back titration.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Masking and demasking`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Masks`,`Note`],rows:[[`Potassium cyanide`,`Zn, Cd, Cu, Co, Ni, Hg`,`Never in acid; HCN evolves`],[`Triethanolamine`,`Al, Fe, Mn`,`Used at pH 10`],[`Fluoride`,`Al, Ca, Mg`,`As stable fluoro complexes`],[`Ascorbic acid / hydroxylamine`,`Fe3+ reduced to Fe2+`,`Redox masking`],[`2,3-dimercaptopropanol (BAL)`,`Pb, Bi, Cd, Hg`,`Thiol masking`]],caption:`Masking hides an interfering ion in a complex that EDTA cannot break.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Demasking releases the masked ion for a second titration; formaldehyde or chloral hydrate demasks zinc and cadmium from their cyanide complexes.`,`Total hardness of water is titrated at pH 10 with EBT; calcium alone is titrated at pH 12 with murexide or calcon, where magnesium has precipitated as hydroxide.`,`Magnesium by difference: total minus calcium.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Argentometric methods compared`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Type`,`Indicator`,`End point`,`pH`],rows:[[`Mohr`,`Direct`,`Potassium chromate`,`Red Ag2CrO4 forms`,`6.5 - 10`],[`Volhard`,`Back, with KSCN`,`Ferric ammonium sulphate`,`Red FeSCN2+ forms`,`Acidic (HNO3)`],[`Fajans`,`Direct`,`Adsorption dye (fluorescein)`,`Precipitate turns pink`,`Near neutral`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why each method has its pH limit`,text:`Mohr fails below pH 6.5 because chromate becomes dichromate, and above pH 10 because silver oxide precipitates. Volhard must be acidic to stop ferric hydroxide forming. Fajans uses fluorescein, which needs enough alkalinity to be ionised, so strongly acid solution kills it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Mohr Chromate, Volhard Ferric, Fajans Fluorescein`,text:`M-C, V-F(erric), F-F(luorescein). Volhard alone is a back titration and alone works in acid.`}]},{topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is conditional stability constants, the pH selectivity they create, and the practical traps in Volhard and Fajans work.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`K'MY = KMY x alpha4`,caption:`K'MY = conditional (effective) stability constant, KMY = absolute stability constant, alpha4 = fraction of EDTA present as the fully deprotonated Y4- form at that pH.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`alpha4 rises steeply with pH: about 10^-11 at pH 4, 10^-5 at pH 8, and 0.35 at pH 12.`,`A conditional constant of at least 10^6 to 10^8 is needed for a sharp visual end point.`,`Because alpha4 is pH dependent, raising the pH raises selectivity for weakly bound metals; strongly bound Fe3+ and Bi3+ can be titrated at pH 2 to 3 where Ca and Mg do not react.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Metal`,`log K with EDTA`,`Minimum pH for titration`],rows:[[`Fe3+`,`25.1`,`about 1`],[`Bi3+`,`27.9`,`about 1`],[`Cu2+`,`18.8`,`about 3`],[`Zn2+`,`16.5`,`about 4`],[`Ca2+`,`10.7`,`about 8`],[`Mg2+`,`8.7`,`about 10`]],caption:`The weaker the complex, the higher the pH needed. This table is the basis of every selectivity question.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Volhard practical detail`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Add a known excess of standard silver nitrate to the halide.`,`Titrate the unreacted silver with standard potassium or ammonium thiocyanate.`,`Ferric ion indicator gives a permanent reddish brown at the first excess of thiocyanate.`,`For chloride, filter off the AgCl or add nitrobenzene to coat it, because AgCl is more soluble than AgSCN and would otherwise dissolve and give a fading end point.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Nitrobenzene is only for chloride`,text:`AgBr and AgI are less soluble than AgSCN, so bromide and iodide need no coating step. Only chloride does. For iodide, add the ferric indicator after the silver, or iodide is oxidised to iodine.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Fajans and adsorption indicators`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Before the equivalence point the AgCl particle adsorbs excess Cl- and carries a negative primary layer, which repels the anionic dye.`,`After the equivalence point excess Ag+ makes the primary layer positive, so fluoresceinate anion is adsorbed and the precipitate turns pink.`,`Dextrin is added to keep the precipitate colloidal with a large surface, which sharpens the colour change.`,`Dichlorofluorescein works down to pH 4; eosin is used for bromide, iodide and thiocyanate and tolerates pH 2.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Mercurimetry and mercuric acetate`,text:`Chloride can also be titrated with mercuric nitrate using diphenylcarbazone, forming un-ionised HgCl2. Mercuric acetate is separately used in non-aqueous titration to release the base from a halide salt.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ammonium thiocyanate is standardised against silver nitrate, which itself is standardised against sodium chloride.`,`Common EDTA assays in the pharmacopoeia: calcium gluconate, calcium lactate, magnesium sulphate, zinc oxide, zinc sulphate, bismuth subsalicylate and aluminium hydroxide gel.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Many drugs are acids or bases far too weak to give a usable end point in water. Non-aqueous titration replaces water with a solvent that exaggerates their strength, letting a weak base or weak acid be titrated sharply.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why water fails`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Water is amphiprotic: it can donate or accept a proton, so it competes with both the analyte and the titrant.`,`Levelling effect: water levels every acid stronger than H3O+ to the same apparent strength, and every base stronger than OH-. Distinctions are lost.`,`Very weak bases (Kb below about 10^-8), such as caffeine and most alkaloids, simply do not give an inflection in water.`,`Many organic drugs are barely soluble in water anyway.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`One-line reason for non-aqueous titration`,text:`Weak acids and weak bases that cannot be titrated in water become titratable because an aprotic or differentiating solvent removes the levelling effect and raises the apparent strength of the analyte.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Classes of solvent`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Behaviour`,`Example`,`Use`],rows:[[`Protogenic (acidic)`,`Donates protons`,`Glacial acetic acid, formic acid`,`Enhances weak bases`],[`Protophilic (basic)`,`Accepts protons`,`Pyridine, dimethylformamide, ethylenediamine`,`Enhances weak acids`],[`Amphiprotic`,`Both, weakly`,`Alcohols, water`,`General`],[`Aprotic (inert)`,`Neither`,`Benzene, chloroform, dioxan, carbon tetrachloride`,`Diluent, no levelling`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The standard titration of a weak base uses glacial acetic acid as solvent and 0.1 M perchloric acid as titrant.`,`The standard titration of a weak acid uses dimethylformamide as solvent and sodium or lithium methoxide, or tetrabutylammonium hydroxide, as titrant.`,`Crystal violet is the usual indicator for the base titration; thymol blue or azo violet for the acid titration.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Acidic solvent for basic drug`,text:`Opposites attract: an acidic solvent (glacial acetic acid) sharpens a basic drug; a basic solvent (DMF, pyridine) sharpens an acidic drug.`}]},{topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable detail is the chemistry of perchloric acid in glacial acetic acid, the acetous perchloric acid ion, the mercuric acetate trick for halide salts, and the indicator colour sequence.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What perchloric acid does in acetic acid`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`HClO4 + CH3COOH -> CH3COOH2+ + ClO4-`,caption:`Perchloric acid protonates the solvent to give the acetonium (acetous) ion CH3COOH2+, which is the strongest acid that can exist in glacial acetic acid.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`CH3COOH2+ + B -> BH+ + CH3COOH`,caption:`B = the weak basic drug. The acetonium ion is a far stronger proton donor than H3O+, so it protonates bases that water cannot.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Perchloric acid is supplied as a 70 to 72 percent aqueous solution, so acetic anhydride is added to remove the water.`,`Acetic anhydride plus water gives two molecules of acetic acid, so no water remains to level the acid.`,`Excess acetic anhydride must be avoided when titrating primary and secondary amines, because it acetylates them.`,`Standardise 0.1 M perchloric acid against potassium hydrogen phthalate (which behaves as a base in glacial acetic acid) using crystal violet.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`KHP is an acid in water and a base in acetic acid`,text:`In water potassium hydrogen phthalate donates a proton and standardises NaOH. In glacial acetic acid the phthalate accepts a proton, so the very same substance standardises perchloric acid. This role reversal is a classic question.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mercuric acetate for halide salts`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`2 BH+Cl- + Hg(OAc)2 -> 2 B.HOAc + HgCl2`,caption:`BH+Cl- = hydrochloride salt of the drug. Mercuric acetate replaces the strong halide anion with the weakly ionised acetate, so the freed base can be titrated.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Halide ions are themselves weak bases in acetic acid and would be titrated by perchloric acid, giving a false high result. Mercuric acetate removes them as un-ionised mercuric halide.`,`Mercuric acetate is not needed for sulphates, nitrates, phosphates or maleates because those anions are already too weakly basic to interfere.`,`Examples of drugs assayed this way: ephedrine hydrochloride, diphenhydramine hydrochloride, pethidine hydrochloride, thiamine hydrochloride.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Indicators used`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Indicator`,`Titration`,`Colour change at end point`],rows:[[`Crystal violet`,`Bases with HClO4`,`Violet to blue-green then yellow-green`],[`Oracet blue B`,`Bases with HClO4`,`Blue to pink`],[`Quinaldine red`,`Bases with HClO4`,`Magenta to colourless`],[`Alpha-naphtholbenzein`,`Bases in acetic anhydride`,`Blue-green to orange`],[`Thymol blue`,`Acids with methoxide`,`Yellow to blue`],[`Azo violet`,`Acids in DMF`,`Red to blue`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Blank correction is mandatory`,text:`Glacial acetic acid has a high coefficient of expansion, so the titre must be corrected for temperature difference between standardisation and assay, and a blank titration of the solvent is always run.`}]},{topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced items concern the theory of solvent behaviour, autoprotolysis, and the specific pharmacopoeial applications with their calculation factors.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Autoprotolysis: 2 CH3COOH <-> CH3COOH2+ + CH3COO-,  Ks = 3.5 x 10^-15`,caption:`Ks = autoprotolysis constant. The smaller the Ks, the wider the usable acidity range of the solvent and the sharper the potential break.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Solvent`,`Autoprotolysis pKs`,`Dielectric constant`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Water`,`14.0`,`78.5`,`Levels strong acids and bases`],[`Glacial acetic acid`,`14.5`,`6.1`,`Differentiates bases`],[`Acetonitrile`,`28.5`,`36`,`Very wide range, differentiating`],[`Ethanol`,`19.1`,`24`,`Mildly differentiating`],[`Dimethylformamide`,`18`,`37`,`Basic, for weak acids`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A differentiating solvent preserves the differences in strength between acids or bases; a levelling solvent destroys them.`,`A low dielectric constant favours ion-pair formation, so titration curves in acetic acid are governed by ion pairs rather than free ions.`,`Sodium methoxide in benzene-methanol titrates weak acids such as barbiturates and sulphonamides; lithium methoxide avoids the gelatinous sodium salt precipitate.`,`Tetrabutylammonium hydroxide gives soluble salts and is used where sodium salts would precipitate and block the electrode.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Applications by functional group`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug class`,`Solvent`,`Titrant`],rows:[[`Alkaloids and their salts`,`Glacial acetic acid`,`0.1 M HClO4`],[`Amine hydrochlorides`,`Acetic acid + mercuric acetate`,`0.1 M HClO4`],[`Barbiturates`,`Dimethylformamide`,`0.1 M sodium methoxide`],[`Sulphonamides`,`Dimethylformamide`,`0.1 M sodium methoxide`],[`Amino acids`,`Glacial acetic acid`,`0.1 M HClO4`],[`Phenols (weak acids)`,`Ethylenediamine`,`0.1 M sodium aminoethoxide`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Carbon dioxide ruins the alkaline titration`,text:`Titrations with methoxide must be done under nitrogen, because dissolved carbon dioxide behaves as an acid in the basic solvent and is titrated along with the drug.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Potentiometric end point in non-aqueous media`,text:`A glass indicator electrode with a modified calomel reference containing lithium perchlorate in acetic acid (instead of aqueous KCl) is used, because aqueous KCl would leak water and potassium perchlorate would clog the junction.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Perchloric acid with organic matter is explosive on heating; never evaporate perchloric acid mixtures to dryness.`,`The equivalence factor is quoted per mL of 0.1 M HClO4, for example 1 mL of 0.1 M HClO4 is equivalent to 20.17 mg of ephedrine hydrochloride.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Ultraviolet and visible spectroscopy measures how much light a solution absorbs. Because the absorbance is proportional to concentration, it is the workhorse quantitative method of the pharmacopoeias.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What is actually absorbed`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`UV-visible light promotes valence electrons from a bonding or non-bonding orbital to an antibonding orbital.`,`The four transitions are sigma to sigma*, n to sigma*, pi to pi* and n to pi*, in decreasing order of energy.`,`Only pi to pi* and n to pi* fall in the usable 200 to 800 nm window, so a drug must contain multiple bonds or lone pairs to absorb.`,`UV region used analytically is 200 to 400 nm; visible is 400 to 800 nm. Below 200 nm oxygen absorbs, so it is the vacuum UV.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Transition`,`Typical lambda max`,`Molar absorptivity`,`Example`],rows:[[`sigma to sigma*`,`below 150 nm`,`High`,`Methane`],[`n to sigma*`,`150 - 250 nm`,`100 - 3000`,`Methanol, alkyl halide`],[`pi to pi*`,`200 - 700 nm`,`1000 - 100000`,`Alkene, benzene, carbonyl`],[`n to pi*`,`250 - 600 nm`,`10 - 100`,`Ketone at 280 nm`]],caption:`n to pi* is the lowest energy but forbidden, so it gives a weak band; pi to pi* is intense.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chromophore and auxochrome`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chromophore: the covalently unsaturated group actually responsible for absorption, such as C=C, C=O, N=N, NO2 or an aromatic ring.`,`Auxochrome: a saturated group with a lone pair which, when attached to a chromophore, shifts and intensifies the absorption. Examples are OH, NH2, OR, SH and halogen.`,`An auxochrome alone does not absorb in the working UV region; it only modifies a chromophore.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The four shift terms`,text:`Bathochromic = red shift to longer wavelength. Hypsochromic = blue shift to shorter wavelength. Hyperchromic = increased intensity. Hypochromic = decreased intensity. Almost every UV question set contains one of these.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`BAtho goes BIG, HYPSo goes SMALL`,text:`Bathochromic - Bigger wavelength. Hypsochromic - Higher energy, shorter wavelength. Hyper - more absorbance; Hypo - less.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`A = log10 (I0 / I) = 2 - log10 (percent T)`,caption:`A = absorbance, I0 = incident intensity, I = transmitted intensity, T = transmittance. 100 percent T is A = 0; 10 percent T is A = 1; 1 percent T is A = 2.`}]},{topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is where the numericals live. You need the Beer-Lambert law in all its forms, the A(1 percent, 1 cm) convention used by the pharmacopoeias, the causes of deviation, and the instrument layout.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`A = a b c   |   A = epsilon b c`,caption:`a = absorptivity, b = path length in cm, c = concentration. When c is mol/L the constant is epsilon, the molar absorptivity, in L/mol/cm.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`A(1 percent, 1 cm) = A / (c in g per 100 mL x b in cm)`,caption:`Specific absorbance: the absorbance of a 1 percent w/v solution in a 1 cm cell. This is the value tabulated in the IP and BP.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`epsilon = A(1 percent, 1 cm) x molecular weight / 10`,caption:`Converts specific absorbance to molar absorptivity. Remember the factor of 10, not 100.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Working absorbance range`,text:`Photometric error is minimum near A = 0.4343 (about 36.8 percent T). Practically, absorbance is kept between 0.2 and 0.8, and dilutions are designed to land there. Outside that range the relative concentration error rises sharply.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Deviations from Beer law`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Cause`,`Effect on plot`],rows:[[`Real`,`Concentration above 0.01 M alters refractive index`,`Negative deviation`],[`Chemical`,`Association, dissociation, complexation, pH change`,`Either direction`],[`Instrumental`,`Polychromatic (non-monochromatic) light`,`Negative deviation`],[`Instrumental`,`Stray light`,`Negative deviation, worst at high A`],[`Instrumental`,`Fluorescence of sample`,`Negative deviation`]],caption:`Beer law is strictly a limiting law valid for dilute, non-scattering, monochromatic conditions.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Beer law relates absorbance to concentration; Lambert (Bouguer) law relates it to path length. Together they are the Beer-Lambert law.`,`Absorbances are additive for non-interacting components at the same wavelength, which is the basis of simultaneous equation (Vierordt) assays.`,`Other multicomponent approaches: absorbance ratio (Q-value) method, derivative spectrophotometry and difference spectrophotometry.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Instrumentation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Component`,`UV region`,`Visible region`],rows:[[`Source`,`Deuterium or hydrogen lamp`,`Tungsten or tungsten-halogen`],[`Cell (cuvette)`,`Quartz or fused silica`,`Glass or plastic acceptable`],[`Monochromator`,`Grating or quartz prism`,`Grating or glass prism`],[`Detector`,`Photomultiplier, photodiode array`,`Barrier layer cell, phototube`]],caption:`Deuterium covers about 190 to 380 nm, tungsten about 350 to 2500 nm.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Glass cuvettes do not work in UV`,text:`Glass absorbs strongly below about 340 nm, so quartz (silica) cells are mandatory for UV. A question that offers glass for a 254 nm measurement is testing exactly this.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Single beam instruments require the blank to be measured separately; double beam instruments split the beam and correct for source drift continuously.`,`A photodiode array (diode array) detector records the whole spectrum at once and is placed after the sample, so the sample sees polychromatic light (reverse optics).`]}]},{topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work covers the Woodward-Fieser rules for predicting lambda max, solvent effects on the two transition types, and the quantitative treatment of two-component mixtures.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Woodward-Fieser rules for dienes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Increment (nm)`],rows:[[`Acyclic or heteroannular diene base`,`217`],[`Homoannular (cyclic) diene base`,`253`],[`Each extra conjugated double bond`,`+30`],[`Each alkyl substituent or ring residue`,`+5`],[`Exocyclic double bond`,`+5`],[`Auxochrome OAc`,`+0`],[`Auxochrome OR`,`+6`],[`Auxochrome SR`,`+30`],[`Auxochrome NR2`,`+60`]],caption:`Sum base value plus all increments to predict lambda max in ethanol.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`For alpha,beta-unsaturated ketones the base value is 215 nm (six-membered or acyclic) and 202 nm (five-membered), with alpha +10, beta +12 and gamma or higher +18 for each alkyl group.`,`Extended conjugation lowers the HOMO-LUMO gap, so more conjugation always means a bathochromic shift.`,`Benzene shows three bands: E1 at 184 nm, E2 at 204 nm and the weak B (benzenoid) band at 254 nm with fine structure.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Solvent effects`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Transition`,`Polar solvent effect`,`Reason`],rows:[[`pi to pi*`,`Bathochromic (red shift)`,`Excited state more polar, better stabilised`],[`n to pi*`,`Hypsochromic (blue shift)`,`Ground state lone pair H-bonded, lowered`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`pH shifts of phenol and aniline`,text:`Phenol at 270 nm shifts bathochromically to 287 nm in alkali because phenoxide has an extra lone pair for conjugation. Aniline at 280 nm shifts hypsochromically to 254 nm in acid because the anilinium lone pair is protonated and lost to the ring.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two-component analysis`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`A1 = ax1 b Cx + ay1 b Cy   and   A2 = ax2 b Cx + ay2 b Cy`,caption:`Simultaneous equation (Vierordt) method. A1 and A2 are total absorbances at two wavelengths, ax and ay the absorptivities of the two drugs at those wavelengths, Cx and Cy the unknown concentrations.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q-absorbance ratio: Cx = [(Qm - Qy) / (Qx - Qy)] x A(iso) / ax(iso)`,caption:`Q = A at lambda max divided by A at the isoabsorptive point. The isoabsorptive (isosbestic) point is the wavelength where both components have equal absorptivity.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Derivative spectrophotometry resolves overlapping bands; a first derivative turns a peak into a zero crossing, and the zero-crossing technique eliminates the interferent.`,`Difference spectrophotometry measures the change induced by pH or a reagent, cancelling irrelevant absorbing excipients.`,`Colorimetric reagents commonly examined: Folin-Ciocalteu for phenols, ninhydrin for amino acids, diazotisation-coupling for primary aromatic amines, Fe3+ for salicylates.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Isosbestic point proves a clean interconversion`,text:`A sharp isosbestic point in a series of spectra means exactly two absorbing species are interconverting with no intermediate. It is evidence of a clean two-species equilibrium, not merely a crossing of lines.`}]},{topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Infrared spectroscopy detects the vibrations of bonds. Because every functional group vibrates at a characteristic frequency, IR is the standard tool for identifying what groups a molecule contains.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Wavenumber (cm-1) = 10000 / wavelength (micrometre)`,caption:`IR is expressed in wavenumbers because wavenumber is directly proportional to energy. 1600 cm-1 equals 6.25 micrometre.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The regions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Region`,`Range (cm-1)`,`Use`],rows:[[`Near IR`,`12800 - 4000`,`Overtones, moisture and blend uniformity`],[`Mid IR (functional group)`,`4000 - 1500`,`Identify functional groups`],[`Mid IR (fingerprint)`,`1500 - 400`,`Identify the exact compound`],[`Far IR`,`400 - 10`,`Metal-ligand and lattice modes`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The fingerprint region`,text:`Below 1500 cm-1 the bands come from skeletal bending and coupled vibrations of the whole molecule, so the pattern is unique to that compound. Two spectra matching there prove identity; this is the basis of pharmacopoeial IR identification.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The selection rule`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A vibration is IR active only if it changes the dipole moment of the molecule.`,`Symmetrical molecules such as N2, O2, Cl2 and H2 show no IR absorption at all.`,`The symmetric stretch of carbon dioxide is IR inactive because the dipole does not change; the asymmetric stretch and the bends are active.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Degrees of vibrational freedom: 3N - 6 (non-linear), 3N - 5 (linear)`,caption:`N = number of atoms. Water (N = 3, bent) has 3 modes; carbon dioxide (N = 3, linear) has 4.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Stretching needs more energy than bending, so a stretch always appears at higher wavenumber than the corresponding bend.`,`Bending modes are named scissoring, rocking, wagging and twisting; the first two are in-plane, the last two out-of-plane.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core of IR is the group frequency table, especially where the carbonyl sits in different environments, plus the sampling techniques and the Raman comparison.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Wavenumber (cm-1)`,`Character`],rows:[[`O-H free (alcohol)`,`3600 - 3650`,`Sharp`],[`O-H hydrogen bonded`,`3200 - 3600`,`Broad`],[`O-H carboxylic acid`,`2500 - 3300`,`Very broad`],[`N-H amine / amide`,`3300 - 3500`,`1 band primary is 2, secondary is 1`],[`C-H alkyne`,`3300`,`Sharp`],[`C-H aromatic`,`3000 - 3100`,`Just above 3000`],[`C-H aliphatic`,`2850 - 2960`,`Just below 3000`],[`C-H aldehyde`,`2720 and 2820`,`Fermi doublet, diagnostic`],[`C-triple-bond-N nitrile`,`2220 - 2260`,`Sharp, medium`],[`C-triple-bond-C alkyne`,`2100 - 2260`,`Weak`],[`C=O (see next table)`,`1650 - 1820`,`Strong, sharp`],[`C=C alkene`,`1620 - 1680`,`Medium`],[`C=C aromatic`,`1450 - 1600`,`Pair of bands`],[`N-O nitro`,`1350 and 1550`,`Two bands`],[`C-O`,`1000 - 1300`,`Strong`]],caption:`Learn the boundaries: above 3000 means unsaturated C-H, below 3000 means saturated C-H.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Carbonyl positions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Carbonyl type`,`Wavenumber (cm-1)`],rows:[[`Acid anhydride`,`1800 and 1760 (two bands)`],[`Acid chloride`,`1800`],[`Ester`,`1735 - 1750`],[`Aldehyde`,`1720 - 1740`],[`Ketone (acyclic)`,`1715`],[`Carboxylic acid`,`1710`],[`Amide`,`1630 - 1690`],[`Conjugated ketone`,`1665 - 1685 (lowered)`],[`Cyclopentanone`,`1745 (raised by ring strain)`]],caption:`Conjugation and hydrogen bonding lower the C=O frequency; ring strain and electronegative substituents raise it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Amide I and amide II`,text:`The amide I band near 1650 cm-1 is mostly C=O stretch; the amide II band near 1550 cm-1 is N-H bend coupled with C-N stretch. Their positions report on protein secondary structure and are asked in biotech-flavoured papers.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`nu (cm-1) = (1 / 2 pi c) x sqrt(k / mu)   with   mu = m1 m2 / (m1 + m2)`,caption:`Hooke law. k = force constant of the bond, mu = reduced mass, c = velocity of light. Stronger bonds and lighter atoms vibrate faster.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Because of the mass term, C-H (3000) is far higher than C-D (2200) and than C-C (1200).`,`Because of the force constant term, C-triple-bond-C (2150) is higher than C=C (1650) which is higher than C-C (1000).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sampling techniques`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Sample form`,`Technique`,`Note`],rows:[[`Solid`,`KBr disc (pellet)`,`KBr must be dry; water gives 3400 and 1640 bands`],[`Solid`,`Nujol mull`,`Nujol masks C-H region 2900 and 1460`],[`Solid or liquid`,`ATR crystal`,`No preparation, most used today`],[`Liquid`,`Thin film between NaCl plates`,`Plates fog with water`],[`Gas`,`Long path gas cell`,`10 cm with mirrors`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Never use glass or quartz optics in IR`,text:`Cells and windows are made of sodium chloride, potassium bromide, caesium iodide or zinc selenide because glass and quartz absorb infrared. Sources are the Nernst glower, globar and nichrome wire; detectors are thermocouple, bolometer, Golay cell and DTGS or MCT in FTIR.`}]},{topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions ask you to separate Raman from IR by selection rule, and to explain the advantages of Fourier transform instrumentation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Raman versus IR`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Infrared`,`Raman`],rows:[[`Phenomenon`,`Absorption`,`Inelastic scattering`],[`Selection rule`,`Change in dipole moment`,`Change in polarisability`],[`Strong for`,`Polar bonds: O-H, C=O, N-H`,`Non-polar and symmetric: C=C, S-S, C-C`],[`Water`,`Absorbs strongly, a problem`,`Weak scatterer, aqueous work easy`],[`Source`,`Broadband thermal source`,`Monochromatic laser`],[`Sample prep`,`Often needed`,`Usually none, can measure through glass`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rule of mutual exclusion`,text:`In a molecule with a centre of symmetry, a vibration that is IR active is Raman inactive and vice versa. Carbon dioxide is the standard illustration: the symmetric stretch is Raman active only.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Rayleigh scattering is elastic and gives no vibrational information.`,`Stokes lines (scattered light of lower frequency) are the ones measured; anti-Stokes lines are weaker because they need a pre-excited molecule.`,`Raman shift is measured relative to the laser line and is independent of the laser wavelength used.`,`Fluorescence is the main interference in Raman; a near-infrared 1064 nm laser (FT-Raman) suppresses it.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`FTIR advantages`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Advantage`,`Name`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`All frequencies at once`,`Fellgett (multiplex)`,`Fast, high signal to noise`],[`No slits needed`,`Jacquinot (throughput)`,`More energy reaches detector`],[`Laser-referenced axis`,`Connes`,`Very accurate wavenumber calibration`]],caption:`FTIR uses a Michelson interferometer; the interferogram is converted to a spectrum by Fourier transformation.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The helium-neon laser at 632.8 nm provides the internal wavelength standard in an FTIR.`,`Polystyrene film is the standard calibration material; its 1601 cm-1 band is the usual check.`,`Resolution in FTIR is set by the maximum mirror displacement: resolution equals 1 divided by the retardation in cm.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Overtones, combination bands and Fermi resonance`,text:`Extra bands appear at roughly twice a fundamental (overtone) or at the sum of two fundamentals (combination). When an overtone happens to coincide with a fundamental of the same symmetry they mix and split, which is Fermi resonance, the reason the aldehyde C-H appears as a doublet at 2720 and 2820 cm-1.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Near infrared spectroscopy is used for at-line blend uniformity, moisture and identity of incoming raw materials in a PAT setting, because it needs no sample preparation and penetrates packaging.`,`IR is a qualitative and identification tool in the pharmacopoeias; quantitative IR is possible but rarely used because of baseline and path-length difficulties.`]}]},{topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`NMR tells you the carbon-hydrogen skeleton of a molecule; mass spectrometry tells you its molecular weight and how it breaks apart. Together with IR they are the standard structure elucidation set.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What makes a nucleus NMR active`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A nucleus must have a non-zero spin quantum number I, which needs an odd mass number or an odd atomic number.`,`Active nuclei include 1H, 13C, 19F and 31P (all I = 1/2). Inactive nuclei include 12C and 16O (I = 0).`,`13C is only 1.1 percent abundant, which is why carbon NMR is far less sensitive than proton NMR.`,`In a magnetic field a spin-1/2 nucleus takes two orientations, aligned (lower energy) or opposed; radio frequency energy flips it.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`nu = gamma B0 / 2 pi`,caption:`Larmor equation. nu = resonance frequency, gamma = magnetogyric ratio of the nucleus, B0 = applied magnetic field. A stronger magnet means a higher operating frequency: 300 MHz for protons at 7.05 tesla.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chemical shift`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`delta (ppm) = (nu sample - nu TMS) x 10^6 / operating frequency in Hz`,caption:`delta is expressed in parts per million so it is independent of the magnet strength. TMS is the zero reference.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tetramethylsilane (TMS) is the reference because it is inert, volatile, soluble in organic solvents, gives a single sharp peak, and its 12 equivalent protons are more shielded than almost anything else, so it sits at delta 0.`,`For aqueous work, DSS or sodium trimethylsilylpropanoate replaces TMS.`,`Deuterated solvents (CDCl3, DMSO-d6, D2O) are used so the solvent protons do not swamp the spectrum.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Shielding and deshielding`,text:`Electron density around a proton shields it, so it resonates upfield at low delta. An electronegative atom or an anisotropic ring current pulls electrons away and deshields the proton, moving it downfield to high delta.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Three things a proton spectrum tells you`,text:`Number of signals = how many kinds of proton. Position (delta) = what chemical environment. Area (integration) = how many protons of each kind. Splitting = how many neighbours.`}]},{topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions demand the proton chemical shift table, the n plus 1 rule with its intensity ratios, and the mass spectrometry rules for molecular ion, base peak and isotope patterns.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Proton chemical shift table`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Proton type`,`delta (ppm)`],rows:[[`TMS`,`0`],[`R-CH3 alkane`,`0.9`],[`R-CH2-R`,`1.3`],[`Allylic CH3-C=C`,`1.7`],[`CH3 next to C=O (ketone)`,`2.1`],[`Benzylic CH3-Ar`,`2.3`],[`CH3 on nitrogen`,`2.2 - 2.9`],[`CH3 on oxygen (ether, ester)`,`3.3 - 4.0`],[`Vinylic C=CH`,`4.5 - 6.5`],[`Aromatic ArH`,`6.5 - 8.0`],[`Aldehyde CHO`,`9.5 - 10.0`],[`Carboxylic acid COOH`,`10 - 13`]],caption:`Alcohol OH (0.5 - 5) and amine NH (0.5 - 5) are variable and exchange with D2O, so they disappear on a D2O shake.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Spin-spin splitting`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The n plus 1 rule: a signal is split into (n + 1) lines by n equivalent protons on adjacent carbons.`,`Relative intensities follow Pascal triangle: 1:1 doublet, 1:2:1 triplet, 1:3:3:1 quartet, 1:4:6:4:1 quintet.`,`Coupling constant J is the spacing between lines in hertz; it is independent of the magnet strength, unlike delta in Hz.`,`Coupled protons share the same J value, which is how you pair up multiplets.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Coupling`,`Typical J (Hz)`],rows:[[`Vicinal free rotation (3J)`,`6 - 8`],[`Geminal on sp3 carbon (2J)`,`12 - 15`],[`Cis alkene`,`6 - 12`],[`Trans alkene`,`12 - 18`],[`Aromatic ortho`,`6 - 10`],[`Aromatic meta`,`1 - 3`],[`Aromatic para`,`0 - 1`]],caption:`Trans coupling is always larger than cis in an alkene, which is how geometry is assigned.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Integration gives a ratio, not a count`,text:`The integral trace gives relative numbers of protons. Ratios of 2:3 could equally be 4:6 or 6:9. You need the molecular formula to convert the ratio into an absolute count.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mass spectrometry basics`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Molecular ion (M+) is the peak from the intact molecule minus one electron; its m/z gives the molecular weight.`,`Base peak is the tallest peak in the spectrum, arbitrarily set to 100 percent relative abundance. It need not be the molecular ion.`,`Electron impact ionisation at 70 eV is the classical hard technique; ESI and MALDI are soft techniques that preserve the molecular ion.`,`Fragmentation happens because the molecular ion is formed with excess energy and breaks at the weakest bonds to give the most stable cation.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The nitrogen rule`,text:`A compound with an even-numbered molecular ion contains zero or an even number of nitrogen atoms; an odd molecular ion means an odd number of nitrogens. Caffeine at m/z 194 has four nitrogens; nicotine at m/z 162 has two.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Element`,`Isotope peak pattern`],rows:[[`Chlorine (1 Cl)`,`M and M+2 in 3:1`],[`Bromine (1 Br)`,`M and M+2 in 1:1`],[`Two chlorines`,`M : M+2 : M+4 = 9 : 6 : 1`],[`Two bromines`,`M : M+2 : M+4 = 1 : 2 : 1`],[`Cl plus Br`,`M : M+2 : M+4 = 3 : 4 : 1`]],caption:`These ratios come straight from the natural abundances: 35Cl/37Cl is 75.8/24.2 and 79Br/81Br is 50.7/49.3.`}]},{topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test named fragmentation processes, carbon-13 features, and the instrument options in modern mass spectrometry.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Named fragmentations`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`McLafferty rearrangement: a carbonyl compound with a gamma hydrogen transfers that hydrogen through a six-membered transition state and expels a neutral alkene, giving an even-electron enol radical cation. For 2-hexanone this gives m/z 58.`,`Alpha cleavage: bond breaking next to a heteroatom, giving the resonance stabilised oxonium or iminium ion. Alcohols give M-1 by loss of H, ethers cleave at the C-O bond.`,`Benzylic cleavage of an alkylbenzene gives m/z 91, the tropylium ion, an extremely common base peak.`,`Retro-Diels-Alder is characteristic of cyclohexenes and of flavonoids.`,`Alcohols very readily lose water to give M-18, so their molecular ion is often absent.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Loss from M+`,`Neutral lost`,`Suggests`],rows:[[`M - 15`,`CH3`,`Methyl group`],[`M - 17`,`OH`,`Carboxylic acid, alcohol`],[`M - 18`,`H2O`,`Alcohol`],[`M - 28`,`CO or C2H4`,`Quinone, aldehyde, ethyl`],[`M - 29`,`CHO or C2H5`,`Aldehyde, ethyl`],[`M - 31`,`OCH3`,`Methyl ester`],[`M - 45`,`COOH`,`Carboxylic acid`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`The nitrogen rule applies to the molecular ion only`,text:`Fragments follow the opposite parity because they are usually even-electron ions. An odd-mass fragment from a nitrogen-free compound is normal; do not apply the rule to fragments.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Carbon-13 NMR`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The 13C shift range is about 0 to 220 ppm, roughly twenty times the proton range, so overlapping is rare.`,`Carbonyl carbons appear at 160 to 220 ppm, aromatic and alkene carbons at 100 to 160, and aliphatic carbons at 0 to 60.`,`Broadband proton decoupling collapses every carbon to a singlet, so the number of lines equals the number of chemically distinct carbons.`,`DEPT-135 distinguishes CH and CH3 (positive) from CH2 (negative); quaternary carbons are absent.`,`Because of the nuclear Overhauser effect from decoupling, 13C peak areas are not reliably quantitative.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mass spectrometer components`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Stage`,`Options`],rows:[[`Ionisation`,`EI, CI, ESI, APCI, MALDI, FAB`],[`Analyser`,`Quadrupole, ion trap, TOF, magnetic sector, Orbitrap`],[`Detector`,`Electron multiplier, photomultiplier conversion dynode`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Resolution R = m / delta m; high resolution MS (R above 10000) gives exact mass and therefore molecular formula.`,`ESI is the standard interface for LC-MS and gives multiply charged ions, which brings large proteins into the m/z window of a quadrupole.`,`MALDI-TOF is used for peptides, proteins and polymers; the matrix absorbs the laser energy and transfers a proton to the analyte.`,`Tandem MS (MS/MS) selects a precursor ion, fragments it in a collision cell, and scans the products; MRM on a triple quadrupole is the basis of quantitative bioanalysis.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Which technique answers which question`,text:`UV shows conjugation, IR shows functional groups, MS gives molecular weight and formula, proton NMR gives the hydrogen framework, carbon NMR gives the carbon count. GPAT frequently asks which single technique best answers a stated question.`}]},{topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Chromatography separates a mixture by making its components travel at different speeds between a stationary phase that holds them back and a mobile phase that carries them along.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two fundamental mechanisms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Adsorption`,`Partition`],rows:[[`Stationary phase`,`Solid surface (silica, alumina)`,`Liquid film on a support`],[`Interaction`,`Surface adsorption`,`Dissolving between two liquids`],[`Governed by`,`Adsorption isotherm`,`Partition coefficient`],[`Classic example`,`TLC on silica, column chromatography`,`Paper chromatography, GLC`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Paper chromatography is partition, not adsorption`,text:`The stationary phase in paper chromatography is the water held by the cellulose, not the cellulose itself, so the mechanism is partition. This is asked almost every year.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The other mechanisms`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ion exchange: separation by charge on a resin bearing sulphonate (cation exchanger) or quaternary ammonium (anion exchanger) groups.`,`Size exclusion (gel filtration or gel permeation): separation by molecular size, with the largest molecules eluting first because they cannot enter the pores.`,`Affinity: separation by a specific biological interaction, such as antigen with antibody or enzyme with inhibitor.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thin layer chromatography`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rf = distance travelled by solute / distance travelled by solvent front`,caption:`Rf is always between 0 and 1 and has no units. It is measured from the origin to the centre of the spot.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The plate is coated with silica gel G (G means it contains calcium sulphate binder) or silica gel GF254 (F means a fluorescent indicator is present).`,`The chamber must be saturated with solvent vapour, otherwise the solvent evaporates from the plate edges and the front runs crooked.`,`Detection: UV at 254 nm, iodine vapour, ninhydrin for amino acids, Dragendorff reagent for alkaloids, sulphuric acid char.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rf and polarity on silica`,text:`Silica is polar, so a polar compound sticks and has a low Rf; a non-polar compound runs with the solvent and has a high Rf. On a reversed-phase plate the order is inverted.`}]},{topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam core is HPLC and GC hardware, the normal versus reversed phase distinction, and the four chromatographic performance equations you must be able to compute.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Performance equations`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`N = 16 (tR / W)^2 = 5.54 (tR / W1/2)^2`,caption:`N = number of theoretical plates, tR = retention time, W = baseline width from tangents, W1/2 = width at half height. Bigger N means a more efficient column.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`HETP (H) = L / N`,caption:`H = height equivalent to a theoretical plate, L = column length in mm or cm. Smaller H means better efficiency.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`k' = (tR - tM) / tM`,caption:`k' = capacity (retention) factor, tR = retention time of analyte, tM = void or dead time of an unretained peak. Ideal k' is 1 to 10; below 1 the peak sits near the void volume.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`alpha = k'2 / k'1 = (tR2 - tM) / (tR1 - tM)`,caption:`alpha = selectivity or separation factor, always taken as greater than 1. alpha = 1 means no separation at all.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rs = 2 (tR2 - tR1) / (W1 + W2)`,caption:`Rs = resolution. Rs of 1.5 is baseline resolution; Rs of 1.0 gives about 2 percent overlap. Pharmacopoeial system suitability usually demands Rs of at least 2.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`HPLC`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mode`,`Stationary phase`,`Mobile phase`,`Elution order`],rows:[[`Normal phase`,`Polar: silica, amino, cyano`,`Non-polar: hexane, chloroform`,`Non-polar elutes first`],[`Reversed phase`,`Non-polar: C18 (ODS), C8, phenyl`,`Polar: water with methanol or acetonitrile`,`Polar elutes first`]],caption:`Reversed phase accounts for the large majority of pharmaceutical HPLC assays.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Isocratic elution keeps the mobile phase composition constant; gradient elution changes it during the run to bring out strongly retained peaks without tailing the run.`,`Pumps are reciprocating piston (most common, needs a pulse damper), syringe (pulse-free but limited volume) or pneumatic.`,`Injector is a Rheodyne six-port loop valve; typical loop is 20 microlitre.`,`Detectors: UV-visible and photodiode array (most common), fluorescence (most sensitive but needs a fluorophore), refractive index (universal but insensitive and not usable with gradients), electrochemical, evaporative light scattering, and mass spectrometric.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`RI detector and gradients do not mix`,text:`A refractive index detector responds to the bulk mobile phase, so any gradient shifts the baseline. It is also temperature sensitive. This is the standard reason given for choosing isocratic conditions with RI.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gas chromatography`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Detector`,`Responds to`,`Comment`],rows:[[`FID`,`Carbon-containing organics`,`Most used, destructive, blind to water`],[`TCD`,`Any species differing in thermal conductivity`,`Universal, non-destructive, less sensitive`],[`ECD`,`Electronegative groups, halogens`,`Extremely sensitive to pesticides, uses Ni-63`],[`NPD / TID`,`Nitrogen and phosphorus`,`Drug and pesticide residues`],[`MS`,`Everything, with identification`,`GC-MS is the confirmatory method`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carrier gases are nitrogen, helium and hydrogen. Hydrogen and helium give faster and more efficient separations; nitrogen is cheap but slower.`,`Columns are packed (2 to 3 m, glass or steel) or capillary open tubular (WCOT and SCOT, 10 to 100 m, far higher efficiency).`,`The sample must be volatile and thermally stable; non-volatile drugs are derivatised, usually by silylation with BSTFA or by methylation.`,`Oven temperature programming in GC is the equivalent of gradient elution in HPLC.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`FID for organics, ECD for halogens, TCD for everything`,text:`FID cannot see water, carbon dioxide or the permanent gases because they give no CH fragments in the flame; TCD can see them all.`}]},{topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced chromatography is band broadening theory, the resolution master equation, and the specialised techniques: HPTLC, ion exchange and gel filtration.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`van Deemter: H = A + B/u + C u`,caption:`H = plate height, u = linear velocity of mobile phase. A = eddy diffusion (multipath), B = longitudinal diffusion, C = resistance to mass transfer. There is an optimum u giving minimum H.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`Cause`,`Reduced by`],rows:[[`A, eddy diffusion`,`Different flow paths in packing`,`Small uniform particles, good packing`],[`B, longitudinal diffusion`,`Solute spreads in mobile phase`,`Higher flow rate, viscous carrier`],[`C, mass transfer`,`Slow equilibration between phases`,`Thin stationary film, small particles`]],caption:`The A term vanishes for an open tubular capillary column, which is why capillary GC is so efficient.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rs = (sqrt(N)/4) x ((alpha - 1)/alpha) x (k'/(1 + k'))`,caption:`The master resolution equation: efficiency term, selectivity term and retention term. Quadrupling N only doubles Rs, so changing selectivity is the more powerful lever.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tailing (asymmetry) factor T = W0.05 / 2f, where W0.05 is the width at 5 percent height and f the front distance from the peak maximum. Pharmacopoeial limit is usually not more than 2.`,`Silanol groups on silica cause peak tailing of basic drugs; end-capping and high-purity base-deactivated silica correct it.`,`Ion pair chromatography adds an alkyl sulphonate or tetrabutylammonium counter-ion to retain ionised drugs on a reversed-phase column.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`HPTLC`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`HPTLC uses 5 to 6 micrometre particles with a narrow size distribution, a thinner layer, automated band application and densitometric scanning.`,`Compared with TLC it gives shorter migration distance (typically 6 to 8 cm), faster runs, better resolution and true quantitation.`,`Densitometric scanning is done in reflectance mode at a fixed wavelength, or by fluorescence.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ion exchange and size exclusion`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Functional group`,`Binds`],rows:[[`Strong cation exchanger`,`Sulphonic acid, -SO3-`,`Cations at any pH`],[`Weak cation exchanger`,`Carboxylic acid, -COO-`,`Cations above its pKa`],[`Strong anion exchanger`,`Quaternary ammonium`,`Anions at any pH`],[`Weak anion exchanger`,`Primary to tertiary amine`,`Anions below its pKa`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ion exchange is used for water deionisation, separation of amino acids, and removal of interfering ions in assays.`,`Gel filtration media: Sephadex (cross-linked dextran) for aqueous work, Sephacryl and Bio-Gel P (polyacrylamide); Styragel for organic gel permeation.`,`In size exclusion, elution volume lies between the void volume V0 (largest molecules, excluded) and the total volume Vt (smallest, fully permeating).`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Largest first in gel filtration`,text:`Size exclusion is the one mode where the biggest molecule elutes first, because it is excluded from the pores and therefore travels the shortest path. Every other mode retains by interaction strength.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Do not confuse HPLC pressure with column efficiency`,text:`Back pressure rises as particle size falls (roughly with the inverse square of diameter), which is why UPLC with sub-2-micrometre particles needs a system rated to 1000 bar. The gain is efficiency, not pressure for its own sake.`}]},{topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Electrochemical methods measure a potential, a current or a conductance and turn it into a concentration. Thermal methods measure what happens to a sample as it is heated. Both give end points where a colour change would fail.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The main electroanalytical methods`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Measures`,`Condition`],rows:[[`Potentiometry`,`Potential (voltage)`,`At zero current`],[`Conductometry`,`Conductance of the solution`,`AC, non-polarisable electrodes`],[`Polarography`,`Current versus applied voltage`,`Dropping mercury electrode`],[`Amperometry`,`Current at a fixed voltage`,`Diffusion limited`],[`Coulometry`,`Total charge passed`,`Faraday law`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Electrodes`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`An indicator electrode responds to the analyte; a reference electrode holds a fixed known potential.`,`The glass electrode is the indicator electrode for pH; it responds to hydrogen ion activity across a thin glass membrane.`,`Saturated calomel electrode (SCE) is mercury with mercurous chloride in saturated KCl, potential +0.244 V versus SHE.`,`Silver-silver chloride electrode is a silver wire coated with AgCl in KCl, potential +0.222 V versus SHE in saturated KCl; it is the reference built into a combination pH electrode.`,`The standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) is the primary reference with potential defined as exactly 0.000 V.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`What makes a good reference electrode`,text:`Constant and reproducible potential, insensitive to the composition of the test solution, and unaffected by small currents (non-polarisable). SCE and Ag/AgCl satisfy all three; the glass electrode does not, because it is meant to respond.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thermal methods in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`TGA measures change in mass with temperature.`,`DTA measures the temperature difference between sample and inert reference.`,`DSC measures the heat flow (energy) needed to keep sample and reference at the same temperature.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`TGA weighs, DTA compares, DSC counts calories`,text:`TGA = mass. DTA = delta temperature. DSC = delta heat, and only DSC gives a quantitative enthalpy in joules per gram.`}]},{topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level covers potentiometric end point detection, the shapes of conductometric titration curves, and the Ilkovic equation with the half-wave potential in polarography.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Potentiometry`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E cell = E indicator - E reference   and   E = E0 + (0.0592/n) log [Mn+] at 25 C`,caption:`A tenfold change in a monovalent ion changes the potential by 59.2 mV; for a divalent ion it is 29.6 mV.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The end point is the maximum of the first derivative dE/dV, or the point where the second derivative d2E/dV2 crosses zero.`,`Gran plot linearises the data and lets the end point be found from points before the equivalence point.`,`Ion selective electrodes exist for fluoride (LaF3 crystal), calcium, potassium (valinomycin) and nitrate.`,`Alkaline error: at high pH the glass electrode responds partly to sodium ions and reads a pH lower than the true value.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Conductometry`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Specific conductance k = (1/R) x (l/a)   and   Equivalent conductance = 1000 k / C`,caption:`R = resistance, l/a = cell constant in per cm, C = concentration in equivalents per litre. Specific conductance is in siemens per cm.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Titration`,`Curve shape`],rows:[[`Strong acid vs strong base`,`V shape: sharp fall then sharp rise`],[`Weak acid vs strong base`,`Small initial dip, gentle rise, steeper rise after end point`],[`Strong acid vs weak base`,`Falls then almost flat after end point`],[`Precipitation (mixture)`,`Two breaks, one per component`]],caption:`The fall happens because fast-moving H+ (mobility 350) is replaced by the slower cation of the salt.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why conductometry wins`,text:`It works with coloured, turbid and very dilute solutions, with very weak acids, and with mixtures of a strong and a weak acid, because the end point comes from the intersection of two straight lines rather than from a single reading at the equivalence point.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Polarography`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`id = 607 n D^(1/2) m^(2/3) t^(1/6) C`,caption:`Ilkovic equation. id = average diffusion current in microampere, n = electrons, D = diffusion coefficient in cm2/s, m = mercury flow rate in mg/s, t = drop time in s, C = concentration in millimole per litre.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The diffusion current is directly proportional to concentration, which makes polarography quantitative.`,`The half-wave potential E1/2 is the potential at half the diffusion current; it is characteristic of the species and independent of concentration, so it is the qualitative parameter.`,`A supporting electrolyte (KCl, KNO3) at about 50-fold excess suppresses the migration current so only diffusion current remains.`,`A maximum suppressor such as gelatin or Triton X-100 removes the current maximum caused by streaming at the drop surface.`,`Dissolved oxygen gives two waves at about -0.05 V and -0.9 V, so nitrogen is bubbled through before recording.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`id gives how much, E1/2 gives what`,text:`Diffusion current is the quantitative term (proportional to concentration); half-wave potential is the qualitative term (independent of concentration). Swapping these is a standard distractor.`}]},{topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material covers why mercury is used as a working electrode, amperometric and coulometric titration, and the interpretation of thermal curves.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The dropping mercury electrode gives a fresh, reproducible, perfectly smooth surface with every drop, and hydrogen has a very high overvoltage on mercury so reduction can be studied to about -2 V.`,`Mercury cannot be used above about +0.4 V because mercury itself oxidises; anodic work needs platinum or carbon.`,`Pulse and differential pulse polarography raise sensitivity from about 10^-5 M to 10^-8 M by sampling the current after the charging current has decayed.`,`Stripping voltammetry preconcentrates the metal into a mercury film and then strips it back, reaching 10^-10 M for heavy metals.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Amperometry and coulometry`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Amperometric titration plots current against titrant volume at a fixed potential; the end point is the intersection of two straight lines, so no reading at the equivalence point is needed.`,`The dead-stop (biamperometric) end point uses two identical platinum electrodes with a small potential across them; current flows only when a reversible couple is present, as in Karl Fischer.`,`Coulometry uses Faraday law: 96485 coulombs per equivalent. Constant current coulometric titration generates the titrant in situ and needs no standard solution.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`w = (Q x M) / (n x F)   with   Q = i x t`,caption:`w = mass deposited or reacted in g, Q = charge in coulombs, i = current in A, t = time in s, M = molar mass, n = electrons, F = 96485 C per mole of electrons.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thermal analysis compared`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Technique`,`Measured quantity`,`Y axis`,`Typical use`],rows:[[`TGA`,`Mass`,`Percent weight`,`Moisture, solvate, decomposition`],[`DTG`,`Rate of mass change`,`dm/dT`,`Resolving overlapping losses`],[`DTA`,`Temperature difference`,`delta T`,`Qualitative transitions`],[`DSC`,`Heat flow`,`mW or mcal/s`,`Melting point, purity, polymorphs, Tg`],[`TMA`,`Dimension change`,`Length`,`Expansion, softening`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Endothermic events: melting, boiling, desolvation, glass transition (a step, not a peak), most decompositions.`,`Exothermic events: crystallisation, oxidative degradation, some polymorphic conversions.`,`DSC purity determination uses the van t Hoff equation applied to the melting endotherm and is valid for purity above about 98 mole percent.`,`A weight loss on TGA that matches one water per molecule identifies a monohydrate; a DSC endotherm with no TGA loss is a true melting or polymorphic transition.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`DSC versus DTA in one sentence`,text:`DTA records the temperature difference between sample and reference and is only semi-quantitative; DSC records the energy required to hold them at the same temperature, so the peak area is directly the enthalpy of the transition.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Glass transition is a step, not a peak`,text:`Tg appears as a shift in the baseline (a change in heat capacity), not as a peak, because no latent heat is absorbed. Melting gives a peak; Tg gives a step. Amorphous solid dispersions are characterised by exactly this feature.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pa-errors-1`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`How many significant figures are there in the number 0.00250?`,options:[`Two`,`Five`,`Three`,`Six`],answer:2,explanation:`Leading zeros are never significant, so the three zeros before 2 do not count. The significant digits are 2, 5 and the trailing zero after the decimal point, giving three. Choosing five wrongly counts the leading zeros, which only fix the decimal position.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-errors-2`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Four titrations agree with each other to within 0.02 mL but all are 0.40 mL higher than the true value. This describes:`,options:[`Good precision and poor accuracy`,`Poor accuracy and poor precision`,`Good accuracy and poor precision`,`Good accuracy and good precision`],answer:0,explanation:`Agreement among replicates is precision, and it is excellent here. Closeness to the true value is accuracy, and a constant 0.40 mL offset destroys it. The offset is one-directional, so it is a determinate error; random error would have scattered the results instead.`},{id:`q-pa-errors-3`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Four replicate titres are 20.10, 20.14, 20.16 and 20.12 mL. What is the relative standard deviation?`,options:[`0.013 percent`,`2.6 percent`,`1.3 percent`,`0.13 percent`],answer:3,explanation:`Mean = 80.52/4 = 20.13 mL. Deviations are -0.03, +0.01, +0.03, -0.01; their squares sum to 0.0020. s = sqrt(0.0020/3) = sqrt(0.000667) = 0.0258 mL. RSD = (0.0258/20.13) x 100 = 0.13 percent. Dividing by n instead of (n-1) is the usual slip and gives a slightly smaller value.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-4`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A sample is weighed by difference. Each of the two weighings has a standard deviation of 0.2 mg. What is the standard deviation of the net weight?`,options:[`0.10 mg`,`0.28 mg`,`0.20 mg`,`0.40 mg`],answer:1,explanation:`For a sum or difference the absolute standard deviations combine in quadrature: s = sqrt(0.2^2 + 0.2^2) = sqrt(0.08) = 0.283 mg. Adding them arithmetically to give 0.4 mg is the tempting wrong answer, but random errors partly cancel rather than add directly.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-5`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The mean of nine determinations is 100.4 mg with a standard deviation of 0.24 mg. What is the standard error of the mean?`,options:[`0.080 mg`,`0.027 mg`,`0.024 mg`,`0.720 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`SEM = s / sqrt(n) = 0.24 / sqrt(9) = 0.24/3 = 0.080 mg. Dividing by n instead of sqrt(n) gives 0.027 mg, which is the classic trap. Note that to halve the SEM you would need 36 determinations, not 18.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-6`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a method error rather than an instrumental or personal error?`,options:[`Reading the burette meniscus from above eye level`,`Using weights that have not been calibrated`,`Co-precipitation of an impurity with the analyte`,`A burette whose graduations are wrongly etched`],answer:2,explanation:`Method errors arise from the chemistry of the procedure itself, such as co-precipitation, incomplete reaction or appreciable solubility of a precipitate. Parallax is personal; uncalibrated weights and mis-etched glassware are instrumental. Method error is the hardest to detect because repeating the analysis reproduces it exactly.`},{id:`q-pa-errors-7`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In an ICH Q2 validation the standard deviation of the response is 0.30 and the slope of the calibration line is 5.0. What is the limit of quantitation?`,options:[`0.06 units`,`0.60 units`,`0.20 units`,`1.67 units`],answer:1,explanation:`LOQ = 10 x sigma / S = 10 x 0.30 / 5.0 = 0.60 units. The corresponding LOD = 3.3 x 0.30 / 5.0 = 0.198, about 0.20 units, which is offered as a distractor. LOQ is roughly three times LOD.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-8`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Precision obtained by the same analyst on the same instrument on different days is termed:`,options:[`Repeatability`,`Reproducibility`,`Ruggedness of accuracy`,`Intermediate precision`],answer:3,explanation:`ICH Q2 reserves repeatability for one analyst, one instrument, one short interval; intermediate precision for within-laboratory variation across days, analysts or equipment; and reproducibility for between-laboratory collaborative studies. A different day inside the same laboratory is therefore intermediate precision, not reproducibility.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-1`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following can be used as a primary standard?`,options:[`Sodium hydroxide`,`Hydrochloric acid`,`Sodium thiosulphate`,`Potassium hydrogen phthalate`],answer:3,explanation:`Potassium hydrogen phthalate is obtainable in high purity, is non-hygroscopic, is stable on drying and has a convenient equivalent weight of 204.2, so it is weighed directly. Sodium hydroxide is deliquescent and absorbs carbon dioxide, concentrated HCl loses hydrogen chloride gas, and thiosulphate is decomposed by acid and bacteria; all three are secondary standards.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-2`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The pH transition range of phenolphthalein is approximately:`,options:[`3.1 to 4.4`,`8.3 to 10.0`,`6.0 to 7.6`,`4.2 to 6.3`],answer:1,explanation:`Phenolphthalein changes from colourless to pink over pH 8.3 to 10.0, so it suits weak acid against strong base where the equivalence pH is above 7. The range 3.1 to 4.4 is methyl orange, 4.2 to 6.3 is methyl red and 6.0 to 7.6 is bromothymol blue.`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-3`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ammonium hydroxide is titrated with hydrochloric acid. The most suitable indicator is:`,options:[`Methyl orange`,`Phenolphthalein`,`Thymolphthalein`,`Alizarin yellow`],answer:0,explanation:`A strong acid against a weak base gives ammonium chloride, which hydrolyses to an acidic solution with equivalence pH around 5.3, so the indicator must change in the acidic range; methyl orange (3.1 to 4.4) fits. Phenolphthalein would change long before the equivalence point and give a large negative titration error.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-4`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`25.0 mL of a sodium hydroxide solution required 20.5 mL of 0.100 N hydrochloric acid for neutralisation. The normality of the alkali is:`,options:[`0.041 N`,`0.122 N`,`0.082 N`,`0.205 N`],answer:2,explanation:`Using N1V1 = N2V2, N(NaOH) x 25.0 = 0.100 x 20.5, so N = 2.05/25.0 = 0.082 N. The value 0.122 N inverts the ratio (25.0/20.5 x 0.1) and is the standard careless error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-5`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The equivalent weight of anhydrous sodium carbonate (molecular weight 106) when titrated with hydrochloric acid to the methyl orange end point is:`,options:[`26.5`,`53.0`,`106.0`,`212.0`],answer:1,explanation:`To the methyl orange end point both protons are taken up (carbonate goes all the way to carbonic acid), so the n-factor is 2 and equivalent weight = 106/2 = 53.0. To the phenolphthalein end point only one proton is added, so the n-factor is 1 and the equivalent weight would be 106.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-6`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the double indicator method, the volume of acid used up to the phenolphthalein end point (P) is less than the further volume needed to reach the methyl orange end point (M). The mixture contains:`,options:[`Sodium hydroxide and sodium carbonate`,`Sodium carbonate only`,`Sodium hydroxide only`,`Sodium carbonate and sodium bicarbonate`],answer:3,explanation:`Phenolphthalein covers all free hydroxide plus half the carbonate; methyl orange then covers the remaining half of the carbonate plus all the bicarbonate. If P is less than M there is extra bicarbonate, so the mixture is carbonate plus bicarbonate. P equal to M means pure carbonate, and P greater than M means hydroxide plus carbonate.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-7`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`0.1 M acetic acid (pKa 4.76) is titrated with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide. The pH at the equivalence point, where the acetate concentration is 0.05 M, is closest to:`,options:[`4.76`,`7.00`,`8.73`,`10.20`],answer:2,explanation:`pH = 7 + 0.5 pKa + 0.5 log C = 7 + 0.5(4.76) + 0.5 log(0.05) = 7 + 2.38 + 0.5(-1.30) = 7 + 2.38 - 0.65 = 8.73. pH 4.76 is the half-neutralisation point where pH equals pKa, not the equivalence point, and pH 7 would apply only to a strong acid against a strong base.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-8`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the Kjeldahl determination of nitrogen, the liberated ammonia is distilled into an excess of boric acid. The advantage of the boric acid receiver is that:`,options:[`The exact concentration of the boric acid need not be known`,`Boric acid oxidises the ammonia to nitrogen`,`It removes the need for a catalyst during digestion`,`It allows phenolphthalein to be used as the indicator`],answer:0,explanation:`Boric acid is too weak to be titrated by standard acid, so only the ammonium borate formed is titrated; an approximate excess therefore suffices and no back titration is required. Potassium sulphate and a copper or selenium catalyst are still needed for digestion, and the indicator used is methyl red, not phenolphthalein.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-1`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Potassium permanganate is described as a self-indicating titrant because:`,options:[`It changes the pH of the solution at the end point`,`It forms a blue complex with starch`,`One drop of excess titrant gives a persistent pink colour`,`It precipitates manganese dioxide at the end point`],answer:2,explanation:`Permanganate ion is intensely purple while Mn2+ is nearly colourless, so the first drop after the equivalence point tints the solution pale pink and no external indicator is needed. The starch complex belongs to iodine methods, and manganese dioxide forms only in neutral or alkaline medium, where it is a nuisance rather than an end point.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-redox-2`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which acid is used to provide the acidic medium in permanganometry?`,options:[`Dilute sulphuric acid`,`Nitric acid`,`Hydrochloric acid`,`Glacial acetic acid`],answer:0,explanation:`Dilute sulphuric acid supplies hydrogen ions without taking part in any redox reaction. Hydrochloric acid is oxidised to chlorine by permanganate through an induced reaction, giving a high result, and nitric acid is itself an oxidising agent that would attack the analyte.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-3`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is an example of iodometry?`,options:[`Assay of ascorbic acid with standard iodine solution`,`Assay of sodium sulphite with standard iodine solution`,`Assay of sodium thiosulphate with standard iodine solution`,`Assay of copper sulphate by adding potassium iodide and titrating with thiosulphate`],answer:3,explanation:`Iodometry is indirect: an oxidising analyte liberates iodine from added potassium iodide and the liberated iodine is then titrated with standard thiosulphate. Ascorbic acid, thiosulphate and sulphite are reducing agents titrated directly with iodine in the burette, which is iodimetry.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-redox-4`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The equivalent weight of potassium dichromate (molecular weight 294.2) as an oxidising agent in acid medium is:`,options:[`147.1`,`49.03`,`98.07`,`29.42`],answer:1,explanation:`Dichromate is reduced from Cr(VI) to Cr(III), and two chromium atoms each gain three electrons, so the n-factor is 6. Equivalent weight = 294.2/6 = 49.03. Dividing by 3 (147.1 would be division by 2) treats only one chromium atom and is the usual error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-redox-5`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A solution contains 3.16 g of potassium permanganate (molecular weight 158) in one litre. Its normality in acidic medium is:`,options:[`0.10 N`,`0.02 N`,`0.20 N`,`0.50 N`],answer:0,explanation:`In acid medium permanganate accepts five electrons, so equivalent weight = 158/5 = 31.6. Normality = 3.16 g per litre divided by 31.6 = 0.10 N. Note that the same solution is 0.02 M, so quoting 0.02 confuses molarity with normality.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-redox-6`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In an iodometric titration starch indicator should be added:`,options:[`Before adding potassium iodide`,`At the very start along with the acid`,`Only when the solution has faded to pale straw yellow`,`After the end point has been passed`],answer:2,explanation:`Iodine is strongly and rather irreversibly adsorbed by starch when it is present in high concentration, which makes the end point drag and gives a high titre. Adding starch only when most of the iodine has been consumed, at the pale straw yellow stage, keeps the blue to colourless change sharp.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-7`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Ceric sulphate is preferred over potassium permanganate for several reasons. Which statement is NOT an advantage of cerimetry?`,options:[`Cerium(IV) can be used in hydrochloric acid medium`,`Ceric sulphate is self-indicating and needs no indicator`,`Ceric sulphate solutions are stable and can be boiled`,`The reaction involves a simple one-electron change`],answer:1,explanation:`Ce(IV) is only pale yellow and Ce(III) colourless, so the colour change is too faint to serve as an end point; ferroin or N-phenylanthranilic acid is required. The other three statements are the standard advantages, along with the absence of intermediate oxidation states and therefore of side reactions.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-redox-8`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the Karl Fischer titration, the role of methanol in the reagent is to:`,options:[`Dissolve the iodine and keep it stable`,`Act as the indicator for the dead-stop end point`,`Provide the water needed to start the reaction`,`Form the intermediate methyl sulphite ester required by the reaction`],answer:3,explanation:`Sulphur dioxide reacts with methanol in the presence of the base to give methyl sulphite, which is the species oxidised by iodine while consuming exactly one mole of water. Without methanol the stoichiometry becomes two moles of water per mole of iodine and the assay is wrong. The end point is detected biamperometrically, not by an indicator.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-1`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Disodium edetate forms complexes with metal ions in a molar ratio of:`,options:[`1 : 3 for trivalent metals`,`1 : 2`,`2 : 1`,`1 : 1 irrespective of the charge on the metal`],answer:3,explanation:`EDTA is hexadentate, providing two nitrogen and four carboxylate oxygen donors, so a single molecule satisfies the coordination sphere of the metal whatever its charge. The 1:1 stoichiometry is what makes the calculation independent of the valency of the metal.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-2`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In a complexometric titration with eriochrome black T at pH 10, the colour change at the end point is:`,options:[`Blue to wine red`,`Wine red to blue`,`Yellow to violet`,`Colourless to pink`],answer:1,explanation:`While metal remains, the indicator is bound to it and the solution is wine red; at the end point EDTA strips the last metal from the indicator and the free blue form appears. Reading the change in the opposite direction is the commonest mistake in this topic.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-3`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A calcium sample required 20.0 mL of 0.05 M disodium edetate. How much calcium (atomic weight 40.08) does the sample contain?`,options:[`40.1 mg`,`20.0 mg`,`80.2 mg`,`100.2 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`Moles of EDTA = 0.05 mol/L x 0.0200 L = 1.0 x 10^-3 mol. Because the complex is 1:1 the sample contains 1.0 x 10^-3 mol of calcium, which is 1.0 x 10^-3 x 40.08 = 0.04008 g, that is 40.1 mg. Doubling to 80.2 mg would wrongly assume a 2:1 ratio because calcium is divalent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-4`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Calcium is titrated with EDTA in the presence of magnesium by raising the pH to 12 and using murexide. Magnesium does not interfere because:`,options:[`Magnesium is masked by potassium cyanide at pH 12`,`Magnesium does not form a complex with EDTA at all`,`Magnesium is precipitated as its hydroxide at pH 12`,`Murexide binds magnesium more strongly than EDTA does`],answer:2,explanation:`Above pH 12 magnesium hydroxide precipitates and is removed from the equilibrium, so only calcium is titrated; total hardness is measured separately at pH 10 with eriochrome black T and magnesium is obtained by difference. Cyanide masks the transition metals, not magnesium.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-5`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a back titration performed in acidic medium using ferric ammonium sulphate as indicator?`,options:[`Mohr method`,`Volhard method`,`Fajans method`,`Mercurimetric method`],answer:1,explanation:`In the Volhard method a known excess of silver nitrate is added and the surplus silver is back titrated with thiocyanate, the first excess of which gives the red ferric thiocyanate colour; nitric acid keeps the ferric ion from hydrolysing. Mohr is a direct titration with chromate at pH 6.5 to 10 and Fajans uses an adsorption indicator.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-6`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the Volhard determination of chloride, nitrobenzene is added before the back titration in order to:`,options:[`Dissolve the excess silver nitrate`,`Keep the precipitate in the colloidal state`,`Oxidise any bromide present to bromine`,`Coat the silver chloride so that it does not react with thiocyanate`],answer:3,explanation:`Silver chloride is more soluble than silver thiocyanate, so without protection the AgCl would slowly convert to AgSCN, consuming extra titrant and giving a fading, high end point. Nitrobenzene coats the particles; filtering off the AgCl achieves the same thing. Bromide and iodide need no such step because their silver salts are less soluble than AgSCN.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-7`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The Mohr method cannot be carried out below pH 6.5 because:`,options:[`Silver oxide precipitates and consumes titrant`,`Silver chloride becomes soluble in acid`,`Chromate is converted to dichromate, delaying the end point`,`The adsorption indicator becomes un-ionised`],answer:2,explanation:`In acid the chromate indicator is protonated and converted to dichromate, so the concentration of free chromate falls and much more silver is needed before silver chromate can precipitate, giving a late end point. Silver oxide precipitation is the limit at the other end, above pH 10, and the ionisation argument belongs to fluorescein in the Fajans method.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-8`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Bismuth can be titrated with EDTA at pH 2 while calcium and magnesium in the same solution are not titrated. The reason is that:`,options:[`The conditional stability constant of the bismuth complex remains high enough at pH 2`,`Bismuth is masked by triethanolamine at low pH`,`Calcium and magnesium precipitate as chlorides at pH 2`,`EDTA is fully protonated at pH 2 and reacts only with trivalent ions`],answer:0,explanation:`The conditional constant K' equals K times alpha4, and although alpha4 is tiny at pH 2, the absolute constant for bismuth (log K about 27.9) leaves K' large enough for a sharp end point, whereas calcium (log K 10.7) and magnesium (log K 8.7) are far too weak. Selective titration by pH control rests entirely on this arithmetic.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-1`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Glacial acetic acid is used as the solvent for the non-aqueous titration of weak bases because it is:`,options:[`Protophilic`,`Aprotic`,`Protogenic`,`Amphiprotic and levelling`],answer:2,explanation:`A protogenic (proton-donating) solvent enhances the apparent strength of a dissolved base, so weak bases such as alkaloids become titratable. Protophilic solvents such as pyridine and dimethylformamide do the opposite job, enhancing weak acids, and aprotic solvents merely act as inert diluents.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-2`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The indicator most commonly used for the titration of a weak base with perchloric acid in glacial acetic acid is:`,options:[`Crystal violet`,`Phenolphthalein`,`Methyl orange`,`Eriochrome black T`],answer:0,explanation:`Crystal violet changes from violet through blue-green to yellow-green as perchloric acid is added and is the standard indicator for this system; oracet blue B and quinaldine red are alternatives. Phenolphthalein and methyl orange are aqueous acid-base indicators and eriochrome black T is a metal ion indicator.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-3`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Acetic anhydride is added to perchloric acid before it is used as a titrant in order to:`,options:[`Acetylate the drug so that it becomes basic`,`Act as a catalyst for the protonation step`,`Increase the dielectric constant of the medium`,`Remove the water present in the commercial 70 percent perchloric acid`],answer:3,explanation:`Commercial perchloric acid contains about 30 percent water, and water would level the acid and blunt the end point; acetic anhydride reacts with it to give two molecules of acetic acid. An excess of acetic anhydride must be avoided when titrating primary and secondary amines, because it would acetylate them and give a low result.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-4`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Mercuric acetate is added when a hydrochloride salt of a drug is titrated with perchloric acid in glacial acetic acid because:`,options:[`It catalyses the reaction between the acid and the base`,`It replaces the chloride with acetate, which is too weak a base to interfere`,`It precipitates the drug so that the end point is visible`,`It prevents oxidation of the drug by perchloric acid`],answer:1,explanation:`Chloride is an appreciable base in glacial acetic acid and would itself be titrated, giving a falsely high result. Mercuric acetate ties the chloride up as un-ionised mercuric chloride and liberates the weakly basic acetate ion in its place. Sulphates, nitrates and phosphates do not need this treatment.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-5`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Potassium hydrogen phthalate is used to standardise 0.1 M perchloric acid in glacial acetic acid. In this medium potassium hydrogen phthalate behaves as:`,options:[`A base`,`An acid`,`An amphiprotic buffer`,`An inert diluent`],answer:0,explanation:`In water the phthalate donates its second proton and standardises alkali, but glacial acetic acid is a far stronger proton donor, so the phthalate accepts a proton and behaves as a base towards perchloric acid. The same substance therefore standardises sodium hydroxide in water and perchloric acid in acetic acid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-6`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Barbiturates and sulphonamides are titrated non-aqueously with sodium methoxide. The solvent used is:`,options:[`Glacial acetic acid`,`Acetic anhydride`,`Dimethylformamide`,`Carbon tetrachloride alone`],answer:2,explanation:`These drugs are very weak acids, so a protophilic (basic) solvent such as dimethylformamide is needed to enhance their acidity; the titrant is sodium or lithium methoxide with thymol blue or azo violet as indicator. Glacial acetic acid and acetic anhydride are used for the opposite case, weak bases.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-7`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The strongest acid species that can exist in glacial acetic acid is:`,options:[`HClO4`,`CH3COOH2+`,`H3O+`,`CH3COO-`],answer:1,explanation:`Every acid stronger than the protonated solvent is levelled to it, so in glacial acetic acid the limiting acid is the acetonium ion CH3COOH2+ formed when perchloric acid protonates the solvent. H3O+ is the limiting acid in water and acetate is the conjugate base, the limiting base of the system.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-8`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For a potentiometric end point in non-aqueous titration with perchloric acid, the reference electrode is modified by replacing the aqueous potassium chloride bridge with:`,options:[`Saturated sodium sulphate in water`,`Molten potassium nitrate`,`Concentrated hydrochloric acid`,`Lithium perchlorate in glacial acetic acid`],answer:3,explanation:`Aqueous potassium chloride would leak water into the titration vessel and, worse, potassium perchlorate is sparingly soluble and would block the liquid junction. A bridge of lithium perchlorate dissolved in glacial acetic acid keeps the junction conducting and free of water, with a glass indicator electrode.`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-1`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A group such as -OH or -NH2 which does not itself absorb in the working ultraviolet region but shifts and intensifies the absorption of an attached chromophore is called:`,options:[`A chromophore`,`A bathophore`,`A fluorophore`,`An auxochrome`],answer:3,explanation:`An auxochrome is a saturated group carrying a lone pair which extends conjugation when attached to a chromophore, producing a bathochromic and hyperchromic effect. A chromophore is the unsaturated group actually responsible for the absorption, such as C=C, C=O or an aromatic ring.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-2`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A shift of lambda max to a longer wavelength is termed:`,options:[`Hypsochromic shift`,`Bathochromic shift`,`Hyperchromic effect`,`Hypochromic effect`],answer:1,explanation:`Bathochromic means a red shift, that is a move to longer wavelength and lower energy, typically caused by extended conjugation or an auxochrome. Hypsochromic is the blue shift to shorter wavelength; hyperchromic and hypochromic describe increases and decreases in intensity, not in wavelength.`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-3`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A solution transmits 25 percent of the incident light in a 1 cm cell. Its absorbance is:`,options:[`0.60`,`0.40`,`0.25`,`1.39`],answer:0,explanation:`A = 2 - log10(percent T) = 2 - log10(25) = 2 - 1.398 = 0.602. Equivalently A = log10(100/25) = log10(4) = 0.602. Quoting 1.39 mistakes the logarithm of 25 itself for the absorbance.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-4`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A drug has A(1 percent, 1 cm) of 300 at its lambda max. A solution gives an absorbance of 0.600 in a 1 cm cell. Its concentration is:`,options:[`2 microgram per mL`,`200 microgram per mL`,`20 microgram per mL`,`2 mg per mL`],answer:2,explanation:`c = A / (A(1 percent, 1 cm) x b) = 0.600 / (300 x 1) = 0.00200 g per 100 mL, that is 0.002 percent w/v. Converting, 0.002 g in 100 mL = 2 mg in 100 mL = 0.02 mg per mL = 20 microgram per mL. Forgetting that the specific absorbance is defined per 100 mL is what generates the other options.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-5`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A compound of molecular weight 250 has A(1 percent, 1 cm) of 400. Its molar absorptivity is:`,options:[`1000`,`10000`,`4000`,`100000`],answer:1,explanation:`epsilon = A(1 percent, 1 cm) x molecular weight / 10 = 400 x 250 / 10 = 10000 L per mol per cm. The factor of 10 arises because a 1 percent w/v solution is 10 g per litre; dividing by 100 instead gives 1000 and is the usual error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-6`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is an instrumental cause of deviation from the Beer-Lambert law?`,options:[`Association of the solute at high concentration`,`Formation of a complex between analyte and solvent`,`Dissociation of a weak acid analyte with pH`,`Use of polychromatic radiation and stray light`],answer:3,explanation:`Non-monochromatic radiation and stray light both cause a negative deviation because the extra wavelengths are absorbed less strongly than the analytical wavelength. Association, dissociation and complexation are chemical deviations, and refractive index change at high concentration is the real deviation.`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-7`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`On changing from hexane to ethanol, the n to pi* band of a ketone shifts to shorter wavelength while the pi to pi* band shifts to longer wavelength. The reason for the n to pi* behaviour is that:`,options:[`The excited state is more polar and is stabilised by ethanol`,`Ethanol absorbs at the same wavelength and masks the band`,`The non-bonding lone pair is hydrogen bonded in the ground state, lowering its energy`,`The n to pi* transition becomes symmetry forbidden in ethanol`],answer:2,explanation:`Hydrogen bonding stabilises the non-bonding lone pair of the ground state, so a larger energy gap must be crossed and the band moves hypsochromically. For pi to pi* the more polar excited state is preferentially stabilised, so the gap narrows and a bathochromic shift results. This pair of opposite solvent effects is a favourite discriminating question.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-8`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Phenol absorbs at 270 nm in neutral solution but at 287 nm in sodium hydroxide. This is because:`,options:[`The phenoxide ion has an extra lone pair available for conjugation with the ring`,`Alkali hydrolyses the aromatic ring and shortens the conjugation`,`The refractive index of alkaline solution is higher`,`Hydroxide ion itself absorbs at 287 nm`],answer:0,explanation:`Loss of the phenolic proton leaves an oxygen with two lone pairs available to conjugate into the ring, which raises the HOMO, narrows the gap and gives a bathochromic shift with increased intensity. Aniline shows the mirror-image behaviour: it shifts hypsochromically in acid because its lone pair is protonated and withdrawn from conjugation.`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-1`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The fingerprint region of an infrared spectrum lies between:`,options:[`4000 and 2500 cm-1`,`2500 and 2000 cm-1`,`1500 and 400 cm-1`,`400 and 10 cm-1`],answer:2,explanation:`Below about 1500 cm-1 the bands arise from skeletal bending and coupled vibrations of the molecule as a whole, so the pattern is unique to a given compound and is used for pharmacopoeial identification. The region 4000 to 1500 cm-1 is the group frequency region and 400 to 10 cm-1 is the far infrared.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-2`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A vibration is infrared active only if it produces a change in the:`,options:[`Dipole moment of the molecule`,`Polarisability of the molecule`,`Nuclear spin state`,`Molecular orbital symmetry`],answer:0,explanation:`Infrared absorption requires the oscillating electric field to couple with a changing molecular dipole, so symmetrical molecules such as N2, O2 and H2 show no infrared spectrum. A change in polarisability is the selection rule for Raman scattering, which is exactly why the two techniques are complementary.`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-3`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An absorption band appears at 5.80 micrometre. Expressed as a wavenumber this is:`,options:[`580 cm-1`,`5800 cm-1`,`2400 cm-1`,`1724 cm-1`],answer:3,explanation:`Wavenumber in cm-1 = 10000 divided by the wavelength in micrometre = 10000 / 5.80 = 1724 cm-1. That position corresponds to a carbonyl stretch, typically an aldehyde or an unstrained ester. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 58000 and is the usual slip.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-4`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A strong sharp band at 2250 cm-1 in an infrared spectrum is most consistent with:`,options:[`A carbonyl group`,`A nitrile group`,`A hydrogen bonded hydroxyl`,`An aromatic C=C`],answer:1,explanation:`The C-triple-bond-N stretch of a nitrile falls at 2220 to 2260 cm-1, a region where almost nothing else absorbs apart from the weaker alkyne. Carbonyl appears at 1650 to 1820 cm-1, hydrogen bonded O-H is a broad band at 3200 to 3600 cm-1 and aromatic C=C at 1450 to 1600 cm-1.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-5`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which carbonyl compound shows the HIGHEST C=O stretching frequency?`,options:[`Acid anhydride`,`Ketone`,`Ester`,`Amide`],answer:0,explanation:`An acid anhydride absorbs near 1800 and 1760 cm-1 because two electron-withdrawing acyl groups share one oxygen, strengthening each C=O bond. Esters follow at about 1740, ketones at 1715, and amides are lowest at 1630 to 1690 because nitrogen donates electron density into the carbonyl and weakens it.`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-6`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Cells and windows in infrared spectroscopy are made of sodium chloride or potassium bromide rather than glass because:`,options:[`Alkali halides give a higher refractive index`,`Glass reacts chemically with organic samples`,`Glass is not transparent to infrared radiation`,`Glass scatters infrared radiation into the detector`],answer:2,explanation:`Silicate glass and quartz absorb strongly across the mid infrared, so no signal would reach the detector. Alkali halides are transparent there, at the cost of being hygroscopic, which is why KBr discs must be prepared from thoroughly dried material and why NaCl plates fog on contact with water.`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-7`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`According to the rule of mutual exclusion, in a molecule possessing a centre of symmetry:`,options:[`All vibrations are both infrared and Raman active`,`A vibration active in the infrared is inactive in Raman and vice versa`,`Only bending vibrations are Raman active`,`All vibrations are infrared inactive`],answer:1,explanation:`Vibrations that are symmetric with respect to the centre of inversion change the polarisability but not the dipole, so they are Raman only; antisymmetric vibrations do the opposite. Carbon dioxide is the standard example: its symmetric stretch is Raman active and infrared inactive, while the asymmetric stretch and bends are infrared active.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-8`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The advantage of an FTIR instrument by which all frequencies reach the detector simultaneously, giving a large gain in signal to noise, is known as the:`,options:[`Jacquinot advantage`,`Connes advantage`,`Doppler advantage`,`Fellgett advantage`],answer:3,explanation:`The Fellgett or multiplex advantage comes from measuring every frequency at once through the interferometer instead of scanning them one at a time. Jacquinot is the throughput advantage from having no slits, and Connes is the wavenumber accuracy provided by the internal helium-neon laser reference.`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-1`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Tetramethylsilane is used as the reference in proton NMR mainly because:`,options:[`Its protons are highly deshielded and appear at high delta`,`It is non-volatile and stays in the tube after the run`,`It reacts with the sample to fix the chemical shift scale`,`Its twelve equivalent protons give one sharp signal at the far upfield end`],answer:3,explanation:`Silicon is less electronegative than carbon, so the TMS protons are unusually shielded and resonate upfield of nearly every organic proton; all twelve are equivalent, so a single intense sharp peak is obtained and is set as delta zero. TMS is also chemically inert and volatile, so it is easily removed after the measurement.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-2`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which nucleus is NMR inactive?`,options:[`1H`,`12C`,`13C`,`19F`],answer:1,explanation:`Carbon-12 has an even mass number and an even atomic number, so its spin quantum number I is zero and it cannot be observed. Carbon-13 has I = 1/2 and is observable but only 1.1 percent abundant, which is why carbon NMR is far less sensitive than proton NMR.`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-3`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`On a 300 MHz spectrometer a signal appears 600 Hz downfield of TMS. Its chemical shift is:`,options:[`2.0 ppm`,`0.5 ppm`,`5.0 ppm`,`20.0 ppm`],answer:0,explanation:`delta = (shift in Hz / operating frequency in MHz) = 600 / 300 = 2.0 ppm. Because delta is a ratio it is independent of the magnet used; on a 600 MHz instrument the same proton would appear 1200 Hz from TMS but still at 2.0 ppm. Coupling constants, by contrast, stay fixed in hertz.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-4`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the proton NMR spectrum of ethyl acetate, the OCH2 signal appears as a:`,options:[`Singlet`,`Doublet`,`Quartet`,`Triplet`],answer:2,explanation:`The OCH2 protons have three equivalent neighbours on the adjacent methyl group, so the n plus 1 rule gives 3 + 1 = 4 lines, a quartet with intensities 1:3:3:1. The CH3 of the ethyl group appears as a triplet, and the acetyl CH3 has no neighbours and is a singlet.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-5`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A molecular ion peak at m/z 181 in the electron impact mass spectrum suggests that the compound contains:`,options:[`No nitrogen`,`An odd number of nitrogen atoms`,`An even number of nitrogen atoms`,`At least one chlorine atom`],answer:1,explanation:`By the nitrogen rule, an odd molecular ion mass means an odd number of nitrogen atoms, because nitrogen is the only common element with an even mass and odd valency. An even molecular mass would indicate zero or an even number of nitrogens. Chlorine would be recognised from an M+2 peak one third the height of M, not from the parity of M.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-6`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A compound shows M and M+2 peaks of almost equal intensity. The compound most probably contains:`,options:[`One chlorine atom`,`One sulphur atom`,`Two chlorine atoms`,`One bromine atom`],answer:3,explanation:`Bromine-79 and bromine-81 are almost equally abundant (50.7 and 49.3 percent), so one bromine gives M and M+2 in a 1:1 ratio. One chlorine gives 3:1 because chlorine-35 is about three times as abundant as chlorine-37, and two chlorines give a 9:6:1 triplet.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-7`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The McLafferty rearrangement requires the presence of:`,options:[`A halogen atom beta to the carbonyl`,`A quaternary carbon adjacent to the charge site`,`A gamma hydrogen and a six-membered cyclic transition state`,`An aromatic ring capable of forming tropylium`],answer:2,explanation:`A hydrogen on the gamma carbon is transferred to the carbonyl oxygen through a six-membered transition state, and a neutral alkene is expelled to give an enol radical cation; 2-hexanone gives the classic m/z 58 fragment. Tropylium formation at m/z 91 is benzylic cleavage, a different and equally common process.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-8`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a DEPT-135 carbon-13 experiment, which carbons give NEGATIVE peaks?`,options:[`CH2 carbons`,`CH carbons`,`Quaternary carbons`,`CH3 carbons`],answer:0,explanation:`DEPT-135 places CH and CH3 above the baseline and CH2 below it, while quaternary carbons give no signal at all because they carry no attached protons. This single experiment therefore sorts the carbons by the number of attached hydrogens and is the standard follow-up to a decoupled carbon spectrum.`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-1`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In paper chromatography the stationary phase is:`,options:[`The cellulose fibre itself`,`The developing solvent`,`Water held in the pores of the paper`,`Silica gel impregnated on the paper`],answer:2,explanation:`Paper chromatography is a partition technique in which the solute distributes between the mobile organic solvent and the water bound to the cellulose; the cellulose is only the support. Calling the cellulose itself the stationary phase would make the mechanism adsorption, which is what happens on a silica TLC plate.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-2`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A spot travels 3.6 cm while the solvent front moves 9.0 cm from the origin. The Rf value is:`,options:[`0.40`,`0.25`,`2.50`,`4.00`],answer:0,explanation:`Rf = distance travelled by solute / distance travelled by solvent front = 3.6 / 9.0 = 0.40. Rf is dimensionless and can never exceed 1, so the values 2.5 and 4.0 come from inverting the ratio.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-3`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A peak has a retention time of 5.0 min and a baseline width of 0.40 min. The number of theoretical plates is:`,options:[`625`,`1250`,`5000`,`2500`],answer:3,explanation:`N = 16 (tR/W)^2 = 16 x (5.0/0.40)^2 = 16 x (12.5)^2 = 16 x 156.25 = 2500 plates. If the width had been quoted at half height the constant would be 5.54 instead of 16, so always check which width is given.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-4`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An unretained solvent peak elutes at 2.0 min and the analyte at 12.0 min. The capacity factor is:`,options:[`0.17`,`5.0`,`6.0`,`10.0`],answer:1,explanation:`k' = (tR - tM)/tM = (12.0 - 2.0)/2.0 = 10.0/2.0 = 5.0. Using tR/tM without subtracting the void time gives 6.0, the commonest error. A k' between 1 and 10 is the desirable working range.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-5`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Two peaks elute at 6.0 and 7.0 min with baseline widths of 0.60 and 0.80 min. The resolution is:`,options:[`1.43`,`0.71`,`2.00`,`2.86`],answer:0,explanation:`Rs = 2(tR2 - tR1)/(W1 + W2) = 2(7.0 - 6.0)/(0.60 + 0.80) = 2.0/1.40 = 1.43. Forgetting the factor of 2 gives 0.71. A value of 1.5 is taken as baseline separation, so these two peaks are very slightly overlapped.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-6`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In reversed phase HPLC using a C18 column with methanol-water as mobile phase, the compound that elutes FIRST is the:`,options:[`Most non-polar compound`,`Compound with the highest molecular weight`,`Most polar compound`,`Compound with the lowest boiling point`],answer:2,explanation:`The stationary phase is non-polar, so polar solutes have least affinity for it, stay in the polar mobile phase and elute first. In normal phase, where silica is the stationary phase, the order is reversed and non-polar compounds come off first. Boiling point governs elution order in gas chromatography, not HPLC.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-7`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the van Deemter equation H = A + B/u + Cu, the A term is eliminated in:`,options:[`A packed gas chromatography column`,`An open tubular capillary column`,`A gel filtration column`,`A reversed phase HPLC column with 5 micrometre particles`],answer:1,explanation:`The A term is eddy diffusion, which arises from the multiple flow paths through a packed bed; an open tubular capillary has no packing and therefore no A term, which is why capillary GC achieves such high plate numbers. Packed and particulate columns all retain an A contribution, minimised by small uniform particles.`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-8`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which detector is UNSUITABLE for use with gradient elution in HPLC?`,options:[`Photodiode array`,`Fluorescence detector`,`Electrochemical detector`,`Refractive index detector`],answer:3,explanation:`A refractive index detector measures the bulk property of the eluent, so any change in mobile phase composition shifts the baseline and swamps the peaks; it is also highly temperature sensitive and relatively insensitive. Photodiode array and fluorescence detectors respond to the solute, and although electrochemical detection needs a conducting mobile phase it is not ruled out by a gradient in the same absolute way.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-electro-1`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The potential of the saturated calomel electrode with respect to the standard hydrogen electrode at 25 degrees C is:`,options:[`0.000 V`,`+0.222 V`,`+0.799 V`,`+0.244 V`],answer:3,explanation:`The saturated calomel electrode (mercury with mercurous chloride in saturated potassium chloride) stands at +0.244 V versus SHE. The value +0.222 V belongs to the silver-silver chloride electrode, 0.000 V is the defined value for SHE itself and +0.799 V is the standard potential of the silver-silver ion couple.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-electro-2`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which technique measures the change in mass of a sample as a function of temperature?`,options:[`Differential scanning calorimetry`,`Thermogravimetric analysis`,`Differential thermal analysis`,`Thermomechanical analysis`],answer:1,explanation:`TGA records weight against temperature and is used for moisture, solvate and decomposition studies. DTA records the temperature difference between sample and reference, DSC records heat flow and gives quantitative enthalpies, and TMA records dimensional change.`},{id:`q-pa-electro-3`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`For an ion selective electrode responding to a monovalent cation at 25 degrees C, a tenfold increase in the ion activity changes the electrode potential by about:`,options:[`59.2 mV`,`29.6 mV`,`118.4 mV`,`244 mV`],answer:0,explanation:`From the Nernst equation the slope is 0.0592/n volts per decade, so for n = 1 a tenfold change gives 59.2 mV. For a divalent ion such as calcium n = 2 and the slope halves to 29.6 mV, which is the distractor here.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-electro-4`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A conductivity cell of cell constant 1.50 cm-1 gives a resistance of 250 ohm with a solution. The specific conductance is:`,options:[`0.167 S per cm`,`0.0040 S per cm`,`0.0060 S per cm`,`375 S per cm`],answer:2,explanation:`Specific conductance k = (1/R) x cell constant = (1/250) x 1.50 = 0.0040 x 1.50 = 0.0060 S per cm. Reporting 0.0040 gives only the conductance without applying the cell constant, which is the usual omission.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-electro-5`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In polarography, the parameter that is characteristic of the substance and independent of its concentration is the:`,options:[`Diffusion current`,`Half-wave potential`,`Residual current`,`Migration current`],answer:1,explanation:`The half-wave potential E1/2 is fixed by the identity of the electroactive species and is therefore the qualitative parameter, while the diffusion current is proportional to concentration and provides the quantitative measurement. Migration current is suppressed by supporting electrolyte and residual current is the background.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-electro-6`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the Ilkovic equation id = 607 n D^(1/2) m^(2/3) t^(1/6) C, the symbol m represents:`,options:[`Molarity of the supporting electrolyte`,`Number of drops formed per second`,`Molecular weight of the analyte`,`Mass flow rate of mercury in mg per second`],answer:3,explanation:`m is the rate at which mercury flows from the capillary, in mg per second, and t is the drop time in seconds; together m^(2/3) t^(1/6) is the capillary constant. C is the concentration in millimole per litre and n the number of electrons, so the equation is linear in concentration.`},{id:`q-pa-electro-7`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A glass transition appears on a DSC thermogram as:`,options:[`A sharp endothermic peak`,`A sharp exothermic peak`,`A step change in the baseline`,`A loss of sample mass`],answer:2,explanation:`The glass transition is a second-order transition involving a change in heat capacity but no latent heat, so it shows as a baseline shift rather than a peak. Melting and desolvation give endothermic peaks, crystallisation gives an exothermic peak, and mass loss would be recorded by TGA, not DSC.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-electro-8`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The dead-stop (biamperometric) end point used in Karl Fischer titration employs:`,options:[`Two identical platinum electrodes with a small potential applied across them`,`A glass electrode against a saturated calomel electrode`,`A dropping mercury electrode against a mercury pool`,`A silver electrode against a silver-silver chloride reference`],answer:0,explanation:`A small voltage is applied across a pair of identical platinum electrodes; current can flow only when a reversible couple such as iodine and iodide is present, so the current stays near zero while water remains and rises sharply when free iodine appears at the end point. The glass electrode arrangement is potentiometric and the dropping mercury electrode belongs to polarography.`},{id:`q-pa-errors-9`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is an indeterminate (random) error?`,options:[`A burette that consistently delivers 0.05 mL less than the graduation indicates`,`Slight variation between readings in judging the first permanent pink colour`,`An impure primary standard used to prepare the titrant`,`Failure to dry the sample before weighing it`],answer:1,explanation:`Random error is small, unavoidable and equally likely to be positive or negative, so it scatters replicates about the mean; judging the exact instant of a colour change is the classic example. A burette that is always 0.05 mL short, an impure primary standard and an undried sample are determinate errors, because each pushes every result in the same direction and can in principle be corrected.`},{id:`q-pa-errors-10`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`An interfering substance in the sample consumes titrant in proportion to the amount of sample taken. The error introduced is:`,options:[`A constant error`,`An indeterminate error`,`A personal error`,`A proportional error`],answer:3,explanation:`Proportional error scales with sample size, so the percentage error stays the same however much sample is weighed and varying the sample weight will not expose it. A constant error, such as the small volume needed to make the indicator change colour, is fixed in absolute terms and therefore becomes relatively less important as the sample is enlarged.`},{id:`q-pa-errors-11`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A titre is recorded as 24.90 mL when the true value is 25.00 mL. The relative error expressed in parts per thousand is:`,options:[`-0.10 ppt`,`-4.0 ppt`,`-0.40 ppt`,`-40 ppt`],answer:1,explanation:`Absolute error = 24.90 - 25.00 = -0.10 mL. Relative error = (-0.10 / 25.00) x 1000 = -0.0040 x 1000 = -4.0 parts per thousand. The value -0.40 is the same error expressed as a percentage; quoting a percentage figure in parts per thousand is the usual unit slip here.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-12`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A result is computed as R = A x B / C, with A = 10.00 (s = 0.05), B = 5.00 (s = 0.02) and C = 2.000 (s = 0.006). The relative standard deviation of R is closest to:`,options:[`1.20 percent`,`0.50 percent`,`0.71 percent`,`0.07 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`For products and quotients it is the RELATIVE standard deviations that combine in quadrature: 0.05/10.00 = 0.0050, 0.02/5.00 = 0.0040 and 0.006/2.000 = 0.0030. sqrt(0.0050^2 + 0.0040^2 + 0.0030^2) = sqrt(0.000025 + 0.000016 + 0.000009) = sqrt(0.00005) = 0.00707, that is 0.71 percent. Adding the three relative deviations arithmetically gives 1.20 percent and overstates the uncertainty.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-13`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Four results are 12.10, 12.12, 12.14 and 12.30. The tabulated Q at 95 percent confidence for n = 4 is 0.829. Applying the Dixon Q test to the value 12.30:`,options:[`Q = 0.80, so 12.30 is retained`,`Q = 0.80, so 12.30 is rejected`,`Q = 0.53, so 12.30 is retained`,`Q = 1.25, so 12.30 is rejected`],answer:0,explanation:`Q = |suspect value - nearest neighbour| / range = |12.30 - 12.14| / (12.30 - 12.10) = 0.16 / 0.20 = 0.80. Because 0.80 is smaller than the tabulated 0.829 the suspect result cannot be discarded at 95 percent confidence and must be retained, however aberrant it looks. Dividing 0.16 by 0.30 instead of by the true range gives the value 0.53.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-14`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Five replicate assays give a mean of 49.06 percent with s = 0.22 percent. For 4 degrees of freedom t = 2.78 at 95 percent confidence. The confidence interval of the mean is:`,options:[`49.06 +/- 0.61 percent`,`49.06 +/- 0.22 percent`,`49.06 +/- 0.12 percent`,`49.06 +/- 0.27 percent`],answer:3,explanation:`CI = xbar +/- t s / sqrt(n) = 49.06 +/- (2.78 x 0.22) / sqrt(5) = 49.06 +/- 0.6116 / 2.236 = 49.06 +/- 0.27 percent. Omitting the sqrt(n) divisor leaves +/- 0.61 percent, and quoting the standard deviation alone gives +/- 0.22 percent; neither is a confidence interval.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-15`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a recovery study a sample assaying 40.0 mg was spiked with 10.0 mg of pure drug, and the spiked sample was found to contain 49.2 mg. The percentage recovery of the added drug is:`,options:[`98.4 percent`,`123 percent`,`92.0 percent`,`82.0 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`Recovery must be calculated on the ADDED amount alone: (49.2 - 40.0) / 10.0 x 100 = 9.2 / 10.0 x 100 = 92.0 percent. Dividing the total found by the total expected, 49.2 / 50.0 x 100 = 98.4 percent, dilutes the shortfall across the whole sample and is the standard way of flattering a recovery result.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-errors-16`,topicId:`pa-errors`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A method gives correct results for the pure drug but consistently low results for the tablet, because an excipient interferes with the measurement. The validation characteristic that has failed is:`,options:[`Linearity`,`Repeatability`,`Robustness`,`Specificity`],answer:3,explanation:`Specificity is the ability to measure the analyte unequivocally in the presence of excipients, impurities and degradation products, so excipient interference is a specificity failure; placebo and forced-degradation samples are run precisely to detect it. Linearity concerns the response over a concentration range, repeatability concerns scatter between replicates, and robustness concerns deliberate small changes in method parameters, none of which is at fault here.`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-9`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which property would DISQUALIFY a substance from use as a primary standard?`,options:[`A high equivalent weight`,`Being hygroscopic and absorbing moisture from the air`,`Stability towards air and heat during drying`,`Ready solubility in the titration medium`],answer:1,explanation:`A hygroscopic solid gains water between weighing and dissolution, so the weight taken no longer corresponds to the amount of reagent and every standardisation is biased. A high equivalent weight is actually an advantage because it makes the relative weighing error smaller, while stability on drying and ready solubility are requirements rather than disqualifications.`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-10`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The largest and steepest pH break at the equivalence point is obtained in the titration of:`,options:[`A weak acid with a weak base`,`A weak acid with a strong base`,`A strong acid with a strong base`,`A weak base with a strong acid`],answer:2,explanation:`A strong acid against a strong base gives a break stretching from roughly pH 3 to pH 11, so almost any indicator changing between pH 4 and pH 10 can be used. A weak acid with a weak base gives virtually no break and cannot be followed with a visual indicator at all; the mixed strong-weak combinations give intermediate breaks confined to the alkaline or the acidic side.`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-11`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`0.500 g of an impure sample of anhydrous sodium carbonate (equivalent weight 53.0) required 16.0 mL of 0.500 N hydrochloric acid to the methyl orange end point. The percentage purity is:`,options:[`84.8 percent`,`42.4 percent`,`90.5 percent`,`169.6 percent`],answer:0,explanation:`Milliequivalents of acid = N x V = 0.500 x 16.0 = 8.00 meq, which equals the milliequivalents of carbonate. Mass of carbonate = 8.00 x 53.0 = 424 mg = 0.424 g, so purity = 0.424 / 0.500 x 100 = 84.8 percent. Using 106 as the equivalent weight doubles the figure to 169.6 percent, which is impossible for a purity.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-12`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`1.00 g of an antacid containing magnesium hydroxide (equivalent weight 29.2) was treated with 50.0 mL of 1.00 N hydrochloric acid, and the unreacted acid required 18.0 mL of 1.00 N sodium hydroxide. The percentage of magnesium hydroxide is:`,options:[`52.6 percent`,`146 percent`,`93.4 percent`,`66.7 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`Acid added = 50.0 x 1.00 = 50.0 meq; acid remaining = 18.0 x 1.00 = 18.0 meq; acid consumed by the antacid = 50.0 - 18.0 = 32.0 meq. Mass = 32.0 x 29.2 = 934 mg, so the content is 934 / 1000 x 100 = 93.4 percent. Using the full 50.0 meq added, without subtracting the back titre, gives the impossible value of 146 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-13`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`How much oxalic acid dihydrate (molecular weight 126) is required to prepare 250 mL of 0.100 N solution?`,options:[`3.150 g`,`1.575 g`,`0.788 g`,`15.75 g`],answer:1,explanation:`Oxalic acid is dibasic, so equivalent weight = 126 / 2 = 63.0. Equivalents needed = 0.100 x 0.250 L = 0.0250 eq, and mass = 0.0250 x 63.0 = 1.575 g. Using the molecular weight instead of the equivalent weight gives 3.150 g, the standard slip when normality and molarity are confused.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-14`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A buffer contains 0.10 mol of acetic acid and 0.20 mol of sodium acetate per litre. Taking pKa as 4.76, the pH is:`,options:[`4.46`,`4.76`,`5.36`,`5.06`],answer:3,explanation:`pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) = 4.76 + log(0.20/0.10) = 4.76 + log 2 = 4.76 + 0.30 = 5.06. Inverting the ratio gives 4.46, and dropping the log term altogether leaves pH equal to pKa at 4.76, which would hold only if salt and acid were present in equal amounts.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-15`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Boric acid cannot be titrated directly with sodium hydroxide in water. It is made titratable by:`,options:[`Boiling the solution to expel carbon dioxide first`,`Adding sodium chloride to raise the ionic strength`,`Adding mannitol or glycerol, which forms a much stronger complex acid`,`Adding an excess of ammonium chloride before titration`],answer:2,explanation:`Boric acid is far too weak (pKa about 9.2) to give a usable inflection, but a polyhydroxy compound chelates the borate and the resulting complex is a much stronger acid that gives a sharp phenolphthalein end point. Expelling carbon dioxide or raising the ionic strength does nothing to the intrinsic strength of the acid, so the break remains unusable.`},{id:`q-pa-acidbase-16`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A mixture of sodium carbonate and sodium bicarbonate required 5.0 mL of 0.1 N hydrochloric acid to the phenolphthalein end point and a further 20.0 mL to reach the methyl orange end point. The volume of acid equivalent to the bicarbonate originally present is:`,options:[`20.0 mL`,`10.0 mL`,`25.0 mL`,`15.0 mL`],answer:3,explanation:`Phenolphthalein registers only half the carbonate, so the whole carbonate corresponds to 2 x 5.0 = 10.0 mL. Of the further 20.0 mL, 5.0 mL completes the carbonate, leaving 20.0 - 5.0 = 15.0 mL for the bicarbonate. Treating the whole 20.0 mL as bicarbonate ignores the second half of the carbonate and is the usual error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-redox-9`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The primary standard normally used to standardise potassium permanganate solution is:`,options:[`Sodium thiosulphate`,`Sodium oxalate`,`Ferrous sulphate`,`Sodium hydroxide`],answer:1,explanation:`Sodium oxalate (or oxalic acid) is pure, stable and non-hygroscopic, and it reacts cleanly with permanganate in hot dilute sulphuric acid at about 60 to 70 degrees C. Sodium thiosulphate and ferrous sulphate are both easily air-oxidised and are themselves standardised against something else, so neither can serve as a primary standard.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-10`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The internal indicator used in the dichromate titration of ferrous iron is:`,options:[`Starch`,`Methyl orange`,`Barium diphenylamine sulphonate`,`Eriochrome black T`],answer:2,explanation:`Dichromate is not self-indicating, so a redox indicator is needed; barium diphenylamine sulphonate changes from colourless to intense violet at the end point, and N-phenylanthranilic acid is the other common choice. Starch belongs to iodine methods and eriochrome black T is a metal ion indicator for EDTA titrations.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-11`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`20.0 mL of a copper sulphate solution was treated with excess potassium iodide, and the liberated iodine required 18.5 mL of 0.100 N sodium thiosulphate. The molarity of the copper solution is:`,options:[`0.0925 M`,`0.185 M`,`0.0463 M`,`0.925 M`],answer:0,explanation:`Because 2 Cu2+ liberate one I2, and that I2 consumes 2 thiosulphate, one mole of copper is equivalent to one mole of thiosulphate. Thiosulphate used = 0.100 x 18.5 = 1.85 mmol, so the 20.0 mL contains 1.85 mmol of copper and the molarity is 1.85 / 20.0 = 0.0925 M. Halving to 0.0463 M assumes a 2:1 copper to thiosulphate ratio, which the two cancelling factors of two rule out.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-redox-12`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`0.200 g of an impure sample of oxalic acid dihydrate (equivalent weight 63.0) required 28.0 mL of 0.100 N potassium permanganate. The percentage purity is:`,options:[`44.1 percent`,`70.6 percent`,`88.2 percent`,`176 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`Milliequivalents of permanganate = 0.100 x 28.0 = 2.80 meq, so the oxalic acid present is 2.80 x 63.0 = 176.4 mg. Purity = 176.4 / 200 x 100 = 88.2 percent. Using 126 (the molecular weight) instead of 63.0 as the equivalent weight halves the answer to 44.1 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-redox-13`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A hydrogen peroxide solution is found to be 0.600 N. Its volume strength is:`,options:[`1.68 volumes`,`3.36 volumes`,`6.72 volumes`,`33.6 volumes`],answer:1,explanation:`Volume strength = normality x 5.6, because one litre of 1 N hydrogen peroxide releases 5.6 litres of oxygen at STP. So 0.600 x 5.6 = 3.36 volumes. Multiplying by 11.2, the factor that applies to molarity rather than normality, gives 6.72 and is the standard confusion.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-redox-14`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Why must an iodometric titration be carried out in a stoppered flask and titrated without delay?`,options:[`Iodine is volatile and iodide is oxidised by air in acid solution, both altering the titre`,`Starch decomposes rapidly on standing in daylight`,`Sodium thiosulphate absorbs carbon dioxide from the air`,`Potassium iodide is hygroscopic and would gain weight`],answer:0,explanation:`Two opposite errors operate: liberated iodine escapes as vapour, lowering the titre, while atmospheric oxygen oxidises the excess iodide back to iodine in acid solution, raising it. Keeping the flask stoppered, cool and dark and titrating promptly keeps both under control; the stability of starch and the hygroscopic nature of potassium iodide are not the limiting problems.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-15`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a bromometric assay, potassium bromate and potassium bromide in acid generate bromine in situ. The excess bromine is usually determined by:`,options:[`Direct titration of the bromine with sodium thiosulphate`,`Back titration with standard potassium permanganate`,`Adding potassium iodide and titrating the liberated iodine with thiosulphate`,`Measuring the intensity of the bromine colour photometrically`],answer:2,explanation:`The excess bromine liberates an equivalent amount of iodine from added potassium iodide, and that iodine is titrated with standard thiosulphate using starch, which converts an awkward volatile oxidant into a sharp classical end point. Bromine cannot be titrated directly with thiosulphate reliably because it oxidises thiosulphate past tetrathionate to sulphate, so the stoichiometry is not fixed.`},{id:`q-pa-redox-16`,topicId:`pa-redox`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A Karl Fischer reagent has a water equivalence factor of 4.50 mg per mL. A 0.850 g sample required 3.20 mL of the reagent. The water content is:`,options:[`1.69 percent w/w`,`0.169 percent w/w`,`16.9 percent w/w`,`2.84 percent w/w`],answer:0,explanation:`Water found = 3.20 mL x 4.50 mg per mL = 14.4 mg. Sample = 0.850 g = 850 mg. Percentage = 14.4 / 850 x 100 = 1.69 percent w/w. The factor must be redetermined frequently with disodium tartrate dihydrate or standard water, because the reagent loses strength on storage; taking a stale factor is the commonest source of error in this assay.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-9`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The buffer used to maintain pH 10 in the EDTA titration of total hardness is:`,options:[`Acetic acid and sodium acetate`,`Ammonia and ammonium chloride`,`Potassium hydrogen phthalate`,`Sodium dihydrogen phosphate`],answer:1,explanation:`An ammonia and ammonium chloride buffer holds the pH near 10, where alpha4 is large enough for the calcium and magnesium complexes to be stable and where eriochrome black T shows its wine red to blue change. An acetate buffer works around pH 4 to 6 and phthalate around pH 4, both far too acidic for these metals.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-10`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Standard silver nitrate solution is stored in amber bottles because:`,options:[`It reacts with the glass of clear bottles`,`It is volatile and evaporates in the light`,`Light reduces it, depositing metallic silver and lowering its strength`,`Amber glass keeps the solution at a constant temperature`],answer:2,explanation:`Silver salts are photosensitive: daylight reduces silver ion to metallic silver, the solution darkens and its titre falls, so it is kept in amber glass and restandardised periodically. Silver nitrate is a non-volatile solid and does not attack borosilicate glass, so neither evaporation nor glass attack explains the practice.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-11`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`50.0 mL of a water sample required 8.0 mL of 0.0100 M disodium edetate at pH 10. The total hardness expressed as calcium carbonate (molecular weight 100) is:`,options:[`16 ppm`,`160 ppm`,`80 ppm`,`1600 ppm`],answer:1,explanation:`Moles of EDTA = 0.0100 mol/L x 0.0080 L = 8.0 x 10^-5 mol, and the complex is 1:1, so the same number of moles of hardness ions is present. As calcium carbonate that is 8.0 x 10^-5 x 100 g = 8.0 mg in 50.0 mL, which scaled to one litre is 160 mg per litre, that is 160 ppm. Forgetting to scale the 50 mL aliquot up to a litre leaves the figure of 16 ppm.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-12`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Magnesium in an antacid was determined by adding 50.0 mL of 0.0500 M disodium edetate and back titrating the excess with 12.0 mL of 0.0500 M zinc sulphate. The magnesium present is:`,options:[`2.50 mmol`,`0.60 mmol`,`1.90 mmol`,`3.10 mmol`],answer:2,explanation:`EDTA added = 0.0500 x 50.0 = 2.50 mmol; EDTA left over and titrated by zinc = 0.0500 x 12.0 = 0.60 mmol; EDTA bound to magnesium = 2.50 - 0.60 = 1.90 mmol, which is the magnesium because the complex is 1:1. Quoting 2.50 mmol ignores the back titre and 0.60 mmol reports the excess instead of the analyte.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-13`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A chloride sample was treated with 50.0 mL of 0.100 N silver nitrate and the excess silver required 15.0 mL of 0.100 N ammonium thiocyanate. The chloride present (atomic weight 35.45) is:`,options:[`177.3 mg`,`53.2 mg`,`248.2 mg`,`124.1 mg`],answer:3,explanation:`Silver added = 0.100 x 50.0 = 5.00 meq; silver back titrated = 0.100 x 15.0 = 1.50 meq; silver that reacted with chloride = 5.00 - 1.50 = 3.50 meq. Chloride is monovalent, so mass = 3.50 x 35.45 = 124.1 mg. Using the full 5.00 meq gives 177.3 mg and reporting the excess alone gives 53.2 mg.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-14`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`For a metal ion indicator to work in an EDTA titration, the metal-indicator complex must be:`,options:[`Weaker than the metal-EDTA complex but still strong enough to be coloured`,`Stronger than the metal-EDTA complex`,`Exactly as stable as the metal-EDTA complex`,`Completely non-reactive towards the metal ion`],answer:0,explanation:`EDTA must be able to strip the metal from the indicator at the end point, so the metal-indicator complex has to be the weaker of the two, usually by a factor of about 10^4 in stability constant. If it were stronger the indicator would never release the metal, giving the blocked or dragging end point seen when traces of copper, iron or nickel are present, which is why those ions are masked with cyanide.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-15`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the Fajans titration of chloride with silver nitrate using fluorescein, the colour change at the end point arises because:`,options:[`Fluorescein is oxidised by the first excess of silver ion`,`The precipitate surface becomes positively charged past the equivalence point and adsorbs the fluoresceinate ion`,`Free fluorescein precipitates once the chloride is exhausted`,`Silver fluoresceinate is more soluble than silver chloride`],answer:1,explanation:`Before the equivalence point the silver chloride particles adsorb excess chloride and carry a negative charge, which repels the anionic dye; once silver is in excess the surface charge reverses and the fluoresceinate is adsorbed as a pink layer on the precipitate. This is why the titration is done in dilute solution with dextrin added, to keep the precipitate colloidal and the surface area large.`},{id:`q-pa-complexo-precip-16`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The solubility of silver chloride in water at 25 degrees C is 1.0 x 10^-5 mol per litre. Its solubility product is:`,options:[`1.0 x 10^-10`,`1.0 x 10^-5`,`2.0 x 10^-5`,`1.0 x 10^-25`],answer:0,explanation:`For the 1:1 salt AgCl, Ksp = [Ag+][Cl-] = s x s = (1.0 x 10^-5)^2 = 1.0 x 10^-10. In 0.010 M sodium chloride the common ion effect drops the solubility to Ksp/[Cl-] = 1.0 x 10^-10 / 0.010 = 1.0 x 10^-8 mol per litre, a thousandfold reduction, which is exactly why argentometric precipitates are washed with dilute electrolyte rather than pure water.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-9`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Benzene, chloroform and dioxan are used in non-aqueous titrimetry as:`,options:[`Protogenic solvents`,`Protophilic solvents`,`Aprotic solvents that act only as diluents`,`Amphiprotic solvents`],answer:2,explanation:`Aprotic solvents neither donate nor accept protons and have low dielectric constants, so they simply dissolve the sample and dilute the reaction mixture; because they do not level the acid or base they often sharpen the potential break. Protogenic solvents such as glacial acetic acid enhance bases, protophilic solvents such as dimethylformamide enhance acids, and amphiprotic solvents such as ethanol do both weakly.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-10`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Non-aqueous titration is chosen for a drug mainly when the drug is:`,options:[`Too weakly acidic or basic to give a sharp end point in water`,`Insoluble in all organic solvents`,`Volatile at room temperature`,`Intensely coloured in aqueous solution`],answer:0,explanation:`Water levels strong acids and bases and cannot differentiate very weak ones, so a substance with Ka or Kb below about 10^-8, such as an alkaloid or a barbiturate, gives no usable break in water; a solvent that enhances the analyte makes the titration possible. Poor aqueous solubility is a secondary bonus rather than the defining reason, and colour is handled by using a potentiometric end point.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-11`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Each mL of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to how many mg of a monoacidic base of molecular weight 300?`,options:[`3.00 mg`,`15.0 mg`,`30.0 mg`,`300 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`1 mL of 0.1 M titrant contains 0.1 mmol of acid, and a monoacidic base reacts 1:1, so 0.1 mmol of base is neutralised. Mass = 0.1 mmol x 300 mg per mmol = 30.0 mg. For a diacidic base the same 1 mL would correspond to only 0.05 mmol, that is 15.0 mg, so the number of basic centres must always be checked before quoting an equivalence factor.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-12`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`0.250 g of a drug required 9.80 mL of 0.1 M perchloric acid, and each mL of the titrant is equivalent to 24.5 mg of the drug. The percentage purity is:`,options:[`92.0 percent`,`96.0 percent`,`98.0 percent`,`104 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`Drug found = 9.80 mL x 24.5 mg per mL = 240.1 mg. Percentage = 240.1 / 250 x 100 = 96.0 percent. Note that a blank titration is subtracted from the titre before this calculation, so ignoring a blank of a few tenths of a mL is what usually pushes such a result above 100 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-13`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A titrant standardised at 20 degrees C is used at 30 degrees C. Taking the coefficient of cubical expansion of glacial acetic acid as 0.0011 per degree, the correction to be applied to a titre of 20.00 mL is:`,options:[`0.022 mL`,`0.11 mL`,`0.22 mL`,`2.2 mL`],answer:2,explanation:`Correction = volume x coefficient x temperature difference = 20.00 x 0.0011 x 10 = 0.22 mL, which is more than one percent of the titre and cannot be ignored. Glacial acetic acid expands about ten times as much as water per degree, which is why the IP insists that non-aqueous titrations be corrected when the working temperature differs from the standardisation temperature.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-14`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`What weight of potassium hydrogen phthalate (molecular weight 204.2) should be taken to consume about 20.0 mL of 0.1 M perchloric acid during standardisation?`,options:[`0.204 g`,`0.408 g`,`0.816 g`,`4.084 g`],answer:1,explanation:`Acid delivered = 0.1 mol/L x 20.0 mL = 2.00 mmol, and the phthalate accepts one proton in this medium, so 2.00 mmol of it is needed. Mass = 2.00 x 204.2 = 408.4 mg, that is 0.408 g. Taking 0.204 g would use only 10 mL of titrant and would give a less precise standardisation.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-15`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In which solvent could perchloric, sulphuric and hydrochloric acid be distinguished from one another in strength?`,options:[`Water, because it is amphiprotic`,`Aqueous ethanol, because it has a lower dielectric constant`,`Liquid ammonia, because it is strongly protophilic`,`Glacial acetic acid, because it is a differentiating solvent for acids`],answer:3,explanation:`A weakly basic solvent does not fully protonate all three acids, so their intrinsic strengths remain distinguishable and the order perchloric greater than sulphuric greater than hydrochloric can be observed. Water and liquid ammonia are both more basic than acetic acid and level every strong acid to the solvated proton of the solvent, so all three appear equally strong there.`},{id:`q-pa-nonaqueous-16`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sodium methoxide titrations must be protected from the atmosphere chiefly because:`,options:[`Carbon dioxide reacts with the methoxide, giving a high blank and a fading end point`,`Oxygen oxidises the methoxide to formaldehyde`,`Nitrogen dissolves in the solvent and dilutes the titrant`,`Water vapour precipitates the drug from the solvent`],answer:0,explanation:`Methoxide is a very strong base and absorbs atmospheric carbon dioxide to form methyl carbonate, so the titre drifts, the blank grows and the visual change with thymol blue becomes indistinct; the burette is therefore guarded and the titration is often done under nitrogen. Moisture is also excluded, but the carbon dioxide reaction is the dominant and rapid one.`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-9`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Cells used for absorbance measurements below 350 nm must be made of:`,options:[`Borosilicate glass`,`Quartz or fused silica`,`Sodium chloride`,`Optical polystyrene`],answer:1,explanation:`Ordinary glass and plastic absorb strongly below about 350 nm, so quartz, which is transparent down to about 190 nm, is required for the ultraviolet region. Glass cells are perfectly adequate in the visible region, and sodium chloride windows belong to infrared spectroscopy, where glass would be opaque.`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-10`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which electronic transition requires the highest energy?`,options:[`n to pi*`,`pi to pi*`,`n to sigma*`,`sigma to sigma*`],answer:3,explanation:`The energy order is sigma to sigma* greater than n to sigma* greater than pi to pi* greater than n to pi*, so the sigma to sigma* transition of a saturated hydrocarbon needs the shortest wavelength, below about 150 nm in the vacuum ultraviolet. That is precisely why saturated hydrocarbons make good transparent solvents, while n to pi* is the lowest-energy and weakest transition of the four.`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-11`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A solution has an absorbance of 0.30. Its percentage transmittance is:`,options:[`30.0 percent`,`50.1 percent`,`70.0 percent`,`3.0 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`T = 10^-A = 10^-0.30 = 0.501, so the transmittance is 50.1 percent; equivalently percent T = antilog(2 - A) = antilog(1.70) = 50.1. Reading 70 percent assumes transmittance and absorbance add up to 100, which is false because the relation is logarithmic, not linear.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-12`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A solution gives an absorbance of 0.45 in a 1 cm cell. Its absorbance in a 4 cm cell at the same concentration would be:`,options:[`0.11`,`0.45`,`0.90`,`1.80`],answer:3,explanation:`Absorbance is directly proportional to path length, so A = 0.45 x 4 = 1.80. Transmittance, by contrast, falls geometrically: it would go from about 35 percent to 0.35^4, close to 1.6 percent. Longer cells are used to measure very dilute solutions, but above A of about 1.5 stray light makes the reading unreliable.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-13`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A tablet extract was diluted 1 to 50 and the diluted solution gave an absorbance of 0.600 in a 1 cm cell at a wavelength where A(1 percent, 1 cm) is 240. The concentration of the undiluted extract is:`,options:[`0.025 mg per mL`,`0.25 mg per mL`,`1.25 mg per mL`,`12.5 mg per mL`],answer:2,explanation:`Diluted concentration = A / (A(1 percent, 1 cm) x b) = 0.600 / 240 = 0.00250 g per 100 mL, that is 2.50 mg per 100 mL or 0.025 mg per mL. Multiplying by the dilution factor of 50 gives 1.25 mg per mL in the original extract. Reporting 0.025 mg per mL forgets to undo the dilution, which is the single commonest mistake in tablet assay arithmetic.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-14`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 2.0 x 10^-5 M solution gives an absorbance of 0.60 in a 1 cm cell. The molar absorptivity is:`,options:[`3000 L per mol per cm`,`30000 L per mol per cm`,`300000 L per mol per cm`,`1.2 x 10^-5 L per mol per cm`],answer:1,explanation:`epsilon = A / (b x c) = 0.60 / (1 cm x 2.0 x 10^-5 mol/L) = 0.60 / 2.0 x 10^5 = 3.0 x 10^4 L per mol per cm. A value of this size marks a fully allowed pi to pi* transition; forbidden n to pi* bands give epsilon of only about 10 to 100.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-15`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A spectrum shows a band at 275 nm with epsilon about 1500 and a much weaker band at 320 nm with epsilon about 20. The weak long-wavelength band is best assigned to:`,options:[`The n to pi* transition of a carbonyl group`,`The pi to pi* transition of the aromatic ring`,`A sigma to sigma* transition`,`Scattering from suspended particles`],answer:0,explanation:`An epsilon of only about 20 marks a symmetry-forbidden transition, and the n to pi* band of a carbonyl is the standard example, appearing at the longest wavelength because it is the lowest-energy transition. The stronger 275 nm band with epsilon in the low thousands is the allowed pi to pi* absorption of the ring; sigma to sigma* would lie far below 200 nm and scattering would raise the whole baseline rather than give a defined band.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-uvvis-16`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the Q-absorbance ratio method for a two-component mixture, the isoabsorptive point is the wavelength at which:`,options:[`The mixture shows its maximum absorbance`,`The two components have the same absorptivity`,`The absorbance of the mixture is zero`,`The first derivative of the spectrum crosses zero`],answer:1,explanation:`At the isoabsorptive (isosbestic) point both components absorb equally, so the total absorbance depends only on the total concentration and is independent of the ratio in which the two drugs are present; that is what allows the total to be obtained from one reading and the ratio from a second. A zero-crossing of the derivative is the basis of derivative spectrophotometry, a different technique for the same problem.`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-9`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`How many fundamental vibrational modes does a water molecule have?`,options:[`2`,`3`,`4`,`9`],answer:1,explanation:`Water is non-linear with N = 3, so the number of modes is 3N - 6 = 9 - 6 = 3, namely the symmetric stretch, the asymmetric stretch and the bend. Carbon dioxide, also triatomic but linear, uses 3N - 5 and therefore has 4 modes; the figure 9 is the total number of degrees of freedom before translation and rotation are removed.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-10`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A spectrum shows a very broad absorption running from about 3300 down to 2500 cm-1 on which the C-H bands sit, together with a strong band at 1710 cm-1. The compound is:`,options:[`A primary amine`,`An alcohol`,`A carboxylic acid`,`A ketone`],answer:2,explanation:`The extremely broad O-H of a hydrogen bonded carboxylic acid dimer spreads from about 3300 to 2500 cm-1 and swamps the C-H region, and it is accompanied by the acid C=O near 1710 cm-1; the pair together is diagnostic. An alcohol gives a broad band but only around 3200 to 3600 cm-1 with no carbonyl, a ketone gives the carbonyl without the broad O-H, and a primary amine gives two sharp N-H bands near 3300 and 3400 cm-1.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-11`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A C-H stretch appears at about 3000 cm-1. When the hydrogen is replaced by deuterium the C-D stretch appears near:`,options:[`4200 cm-1`,`3000 cm-1`,`2200 cm-1`,`1500 cm-1`],answer:2,explanation:`By Hooke law the wavenumber varies as 1/sqrt(mu). Reduced mass for C-H = (12 x 1)/13 = 0.923 and for C-D = (12 x 2)/14 = 1.714, so the ratio of wavenumbers is sqrt(0.923/1.714) = sqrt(0.538) = 0.734, giving 3000 x 0.734, about 2200 cm-1. Only the mass changes here, since the force constant of the bond is essentially unaltered by isotopic substitution; this shift is exactly why deuteration is used to confirm band assignments.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-12`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Radiation of wavenumber 2000 cm-1 has a frequency of about:`,options:[`6 x 10^13 Hz`,`6 x 10^10 Hz`,`6 x 10^16 Hz`,`1.5 x 10^7 Hz`],answer:0,explanation:`Frequency = velocity of light x wavenumber = 3 x 10^10 cm per second x 2000 cm-1 = 6 x 10^13 Hz. Using the velocity of light in metres per second without converting the wavenumber to per metre gives an answer a hundredfold too small. Wavenumber is used in infrared work precisely because it is directly proportional to both frequency and energy.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-13`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A Nujol mull is unsuitable for examining which part of the spectrum?`,options:[`The carbonyl region around 1700 cm-1`,`The C-H stretching and bending regions near 2900, 1460 and 1375 cm-1`,`The O-H region above 3400 cm-1`,`The fingerprint region below 1000 cm-1`],answer:1,explanation:`Nujol is liquid paraffin, a hydrocarbon, so its own strong C-H bands near 2900, 1460 and 1375 cm-1 obscure exactly those absorptions of the sample. The standard remedy is to re-run the sample in hexachlorobutadiene or fluorolube, which has no C-H bonds, or to prepare a potassium bromide disc instead.`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-14`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A spectrum shows a single sharp band at 3300 cm-1, a strong band at 1660 cm-1 and another strong band at 1550 cm-1. The compound is most likely:`,options:[`An ester`,`A secondary amide`,`A primary amine`,`A carboxylic acid`],answer:1,explanation:`One N-H stretch (a primary amide or amine would give two), the amide I carbonyl near 1660 cm-1 and the amide II N-H bending band near 1550 cm-1 together define a secondary amide, the linkage found in paracetamol and in the local anaesthetic anilides. An ester would absorb near 1740 cm-1 with strong C-O bands at 1100 to 1300 cm-1 and no N-H at all.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-15`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The fundamental C=O stretch of a ketone lies at 1715 cm-1. Its first overtone would be expected near:`,options:[`857 cm-1`,`1715 cm-1`,`3430 cm-1`,`5145 cm-1`],answer:2,explanation:`An overtone appears at approximately twice the fundamental, so 2 x 1715 = 3430 cm-1, though anharmonicity places the real band slightly below that value and its intensity is only a small fraction of the fundamental. Overtones and combination bands are why near infrared spectra are weak and heavily overlapped, and why a weak band near 3400 cm-1 in a ketone must not be misread as an O-H.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-ir-raman-16`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Conjugation of a carbonyl group with an adjacent C=C lowers its stretching frequency because:`,options:[`Delocalisation gives the C=O partial single bond character, reducing the force constant`,`The reduced mass of the oscillator is increased by the extra atoms`,`Conjugation makes the vibration infrared inactive`,`The dipole moment of the carbonyl is increased`],answer:0,explanation:`Resonance spreads electron density from the C=C into the carbonyl, so the C=O bond order falls, k falls and the band drops by roughly 20 to 40 cm-1, for example from 1715 to about 1685 cm-1. The opposite happens with ring strain: in cyclopentanone the band rises to about 1745 and in cyclobutanone to about 1780 cm-1, because the constrained angle stiffens the bond.`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-9`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Deuterated chloroform rather than ordinary chloroform is used as the solvent in proton NMR because:`,options:[`It dissolves organic compounds better than chloroform`,`It contains almost no protons, so it does not add an intense solvent signal`,`It sharpens the signals by increasing the relaxation time`,`It reacts with the sample to lock the field`],answer:1,explanation:`A solvent present in vast excess would produce a signal that swamps the sample, so the hydrogens are replaced by deuterium, which resonates far outside the proton range; the residual CHCl3 still shows a small peak at delta 7.26. The deuterium also supplies the lock signal that keeps the field stable, which is a bonus rather than the reason for the choice.`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-10`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The area under a proton NMR signal is proportional to:`,options:[`The number of protons giving rise to that signal`,`The number of neighbouring protons`,`The electronegativity of the attached atom`,`The coupling constant of the signal`],answer:0,explanation:`Integration counts protons, so the step heights give the relative numbers in each environment and are read as a ratio, not as absolute numbers. The number of neighbours governs multiplicity through the n plus 1 rule, and electronegativity governs the chemical shift by deshielding; neither affects the area.`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-11`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A compound with 10 protons gives three signals whose integral heights measure 25, 10 and 15 mm. The numbers of protons in the three environments are:`,options:[`25, 10 and 15`,`10, 4 and 6`,`5, 2 and 3`,`2, 1 and 3`],answer:2,explanation:`Total integral = 25 + 10 + 15 = 50 mm for 10 protons, so 5 mm corresponds to one proton. Dividing gives 25/5 = 5, 10/5 = 2 and 15/5 = 3 protons. A 5:2:3 pattern of an aromatic multiplet, a quartet-like CH2 and a CH3 is exactly what ethyl benzoate or benzyl acetate would show.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-12`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A coupling constant measured as 7.0 Hz on a 300 MHz spectrometer will, on a 600 MHz spectrometer, appear as:`,options:[`14.0 Hz`,`7.0 Hz`,`3.5 Hz`,`0.023 ppm`],answer:1,explanation:`J depends only on the number and geometry of the bonds between the coupled nuclei, not on the applied field, so it stays at 7.0 Hz on any magnet. What does double is the separation between two chemically shifted signals in hertz, which is why higher-field instruments spread out overlapping multiplets and simplify second-order patterns into first-order ones.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-13`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In an isopropyl group, the methine proton of CH(CH3)2 appears as a:`,options:[`Doublet`,`Quartet`,`Triplet`,`Septet`],answer:3,explanation:`The methine proton has six equivalent neighbours on the two methyl groups, so the n plus 1 rule gives 6 + 1 = 7 lines, a septet whose outer lines are often too weak to see. The six methyl protons in turn see one neighbour and appear as a doublet of relative area 6, and the doublet plus septet pair is the standard signature of an isopropyl group.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-14`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Paracetamol has the molecular formula C8H9NO2. Its molecular ion appears at m/z:`,options:[`150`,`151`,`152`,`137`],answer:1,explanation:`Using the masses of the most abundant isotopes: carbon 8 x 12 = 96, hydrogen 9 x 1 = 9, nitrogen 14 and oxygen 2 x 16 = 32, giving 96 + 9 + 14 + 32 = 151. The odd value is consistent with the nitrogen rule, since the molecule contains one nitrogen atom; an even value such as 150 would demand zero or an even number of nitrogens.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-15`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A hydrocarbon shows an M+1 peak whose intensity is 6.6 percent of the molecular ion. The number of carbon atoms is:`,options:[`5`,`6`,`7`,`66`],answer:1,explanation:`Carbon-13 is 1.1 percent abundant, so each carbon contributes about 1.1 percent to the M+1 peak; the number of carbons is 6.6 / 1.1 = 6. This isotope-counting method works only when the M+1 peak is measured accurately and no other M+1 contributors such as nitrogen or sulphur are present, and it is the classical way of fixing a molecular formula before high-resolution instruments were available.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-nmr-ms-16`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which ionisation technique is soft enough for thermally labile drugs and is the standard interface for LC-MS?`,options:[`Electron impact at 70 eV`,`Electrospray ionisation`,`Field desorption from an emitter wire`,`Fast atom bombardment with a glycerol matrix`],answer:1,explanation:`Electrospray forms ions directly from the flowing liquid at atmospheric pressure, so it accepts an HPLC eluent, imparts very little internal energy and gives an intact protonated or deprotonated molecule, often multiply charged for peptides. Electron impact at 70 eV is the hardest common technique and frequently destroys the molecular ion entirely, which is why it is used for library-matched fragmentation rather than for molecular weight determination of labile drugs.`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-9`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`On a silica TLC plate, the component with the strongest affinity for the stationary phase will show:`,options:[`The highest Rf value`,`The lowest Rf value`,`An Rf value greater than 1`,`No spot at all`],answer:1,explanation:`Silica is polar, so a strongly adsorbed polar solute spends most of its time held on the plate, moves only a short distance and therefore has a low Rf. A weakly held non-polar solute travels close to the solvent front and gives a high Rf; an Rf above 1 is impossible because the solute cannot outrun the mobile phase that carries it.`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-10`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which chromatographic detector destroys the sample, so that no fraction can be collected after it?`,options:[`Thermal conductivity detector`,`Refractive index detector`,`Flame ionisation detector`,`Ultraviolet absorbance detector`],answer:2,explanation:`The flame ionisation detector burns the eluate in a hydrogen-air flame and measures the ions produced, so the sample is consumed; it is highly sensitive to organic compounds but blind to water, carbon dioxide and other non-combustible species. Thermal conductivity, refractive index and ultraviolet detectors all measure a physical property without altering the sample and are therefore non-destructive.`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-11`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A peak has a retention time of 6.0 min and a width at half height of 0.20 min. The number of theoretical plates is about:`,options:[`900`,`1440`,`4990`,`14400`],answer:2,explanation:`For the width at half height the constant is 5.54, not 16: N = 5.54 (tR / W1/2)^2 = 5.54 x (6.0/0.20)^2 = 5.54 x 30^2 = 5.54 x 900 = 4986, about 4990 plates. Using 16 with the half-height width gives 14400 and badly overstates the efficiency, so the width quoted must always be checked.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-12`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 25 cm column gives 5000 theoretical plates. Its HETP is:`,options:[`0.05 mm`,`0.20 mm`,`0.005 mm`,`200 mm`],answer:0,explanation:`HETP = L / N = 250 mm / 5000 = 0.05 mm, that is 50 micrometre, which is about ten particle diameters for a 5 micrometre packing. Forgetting to convert 25 cm to 250 mm gives 0.005 mm; a smaller HETP always means a more efficient column, so HETP and N move in opposite directions.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-13`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Two peaks elute at 8.0 and 12.0 min and the unretained peak elutes at 2.0 min. The selectivity factor alpha is:`,options:[`1.50`,`1.67`,`2.50`,`0.60`],answer:1,explanation:`k1' = (8.0 - 2.0)/2.0 = 3.0 and k2' = (12.0 - 2.0)/2.0 = 5.0, so alpha = 5.0/3.0 = 1.67. Taking the ratio of the raw retention times, 12.0/8.0 = 1.50, ignores the void time and always understates the true selectivity; alpha is defined to be greater than 1, and alpha = 1 means no separation at all.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-14`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A spot on a TLC plate has an Rf of 0.25. The corresponding retention (capacity) factor k is:`,options:[`0.33`,`0.75`,`3.0`,`4.0`],answer:2,explanation:`k = (1 - Rf)/Rf = (1 - 0.25)/0.25 = 0.75/0.25 = 3.0, meaning the solute spends three times as long on the stationary phase as in the mobile phase. Inverting the expression gives 0.33; note that a large Rf corresponds to a small k, so the two measures run in opposite directions.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-15`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`If the column length is doubled while particle size, flow rate and mobile phase are unchanged, the resolution increases by a factor of about:`,options:[`1.41`,`2.00`,`4.00`,`1.00`],answer:0,explanation:`Doubling L doubles N, and Rs is proportional to sqrt(N), so Rs rises by sqrt(2) = 1.41 while the analysis time and the back pressure both double. That poor return is why the master equation is exploited through the selectivity term instead: a small change in mobile phase or stationary phase chemistry that alters alpha buys far more resolution than a longer column.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-chromatography-16`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A basic drug gives a peak with a sharp leading edge and a long trailing tail on a C18 column at pH 7. The most likely cause is:`,options:[`Injection of too large a mass of a very soluble sample`,`Interaction of the protonated base with residual free silanol groups`,`A detector time constant set too low`,`Air trapped in the pump head`],answer:1,explanation:`Acidic silanol groups left unbonded on the silica surface act as a strong secondary ion-exchange site for protonated amines, giving a slow desorption tail; the remedies are an end-capped or hybrid column, lowering the pH to about 2.5 to 3, or adding a competing amine such as triethylamine. Column overload gives the opposite shape, a leading front with a sharp back edge, and pump or detector faults distort the whole chromatogram rather than one peak.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pa-electro-9`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The glass electrode used in pH measurement responds to:`,options:[`The total ionic strength of the solution`,`The chloride ion activity`,`The hydrogen ion activity`,`The dissolved oxygen concentration`],answer:2,explanation:`A thin lithium or sodium silicate membrane develops a potential that varies with the hydrogen ion activity on either side, following the Nernst slope of 59.2 mV per pH unit at 25 degrees C. The internal silver-silver chloride element is what senses chloride, but it sits in a solution of fixed chloride concentration so it contributes only a constant term.`},{id:`q-pa-electro-10`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The asymmetry potential of a glass electrode is dealt with by:`,options:[`Calibrating the meter with standard buffer solutions before use`,`Replacing the internal filling solution each day`,`Applying a small external voltage across the membrane`,`Warming the electrode to 40 degrees C before measurement`],answer:0,explanation:`The two faces of the membrane are never quite identical, so a small potential persists even when the solutions on both sides are the same and it drifts as the electrode ages. Calibration against two standard buffers that bracket the sample pH absorbs both this offset and any slope error, which is why a pH meter must be standardised and not merely switched on.`},{id:`q-pa-electro-11`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A conductivity cell filled with 0.1 N potassium chloride, whose specific conductance is 0.0129 S per cm, gives a resistance of 215 ohm. The cell constant is:`,options:[`0.36 cm-1`,`2.77 cm-1`,`0.000060 cm-1`,`16667 cm-1`],answer:1,explanation:`Cell constant = specific conductance x resistance = 0.0129 S per cm x 215 ohm = 2.77 cm-1. The cell constant is the geometric factor l/a of the electrodes and is determined once with a standard potassium chloride solution, then used with every later reading; dividing instead of multiplying gives the meaningless value 0.000060.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-electro-12`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 0.05 N solution has a specific conductance of 0.0060 S per cm. Its equivalent conductance is:`,options:[`12 S cm2 per equivalent`,`120 S cm2 per equivalent`,`0.12 S cm2 per equivalent`,`1200 S cm2 per equivalent`],answer:1,explanation:`Equivalent conductance = 1000 k / C = 1000 x 0.0060 / 0.05 = 6.0 / 0.05 = 120 S cm2 per equivalent. The factor of 1000 converts litres to cubic centimetres; omitting it gives 0.12. Specific conductance falls on dilution while equivalent conductance rises, because the latter refers to a fixed quantity of electrolyte rather than a fixed volume.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-electro-13`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The potential of a silver electrode dipping in 0.001 M silver nitrate at 25 degrees C, given E0 = +0.799 V, is:`,options:[`+0.799 V`,`+0.621 V`,`+0.977 V`,`+0.740 V`],answer:1,explanation:`E = E0 + (0.0592/n) log [Ag+] = 0.799 + 0.0592 x log(10^-3) = 0.799 + 0.0592 x (-3) = 0.799 - 0.178 = 0.621 V. Because n = 1, each tenfold dilution costs 59.2 mV. Getting +0.977 V comes from adding rather than subtracting the log term, which would imply that dilution makes silver easier to reduce.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-electro-14`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`How much charge is required to deposit one mole of copper from a solution of copper(II) ions?`,options:[`96485 C`,`48242 C`,`192970 C`,`2 C`],answer:2,explanation:`Copper(II) needs two electrons per atom, so Q = n x F = 2 x 96485 = 192970 coulombs per mole. At a constant current of 1.00 A that would take 192970 seconds, about 53.6 hours; this is the basis of coulometry, where the charge itself, rather than a titrant volume, measures the analyte.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pa-electro-15`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In strongly alkaline solutions containing sodium ions, an ordinary glass electrode shows an alkaline error, meaning that the measured pH is:`,options:[`Higher than the true pH`,`Lower than the true pH`,`Unaffected but very slow to stabilise`,`Exactly 14 regardless of the sample`],answer:1,explanation:`Above about pH 9 the hydrogen ion activity is so low that the membrane begins to respond to sodium ions as well, so the electrode reports more acidity than is really present and reads low. Lithium glass membranes push this error above pH 12, and the mirror-image acid error, a reading that is too high, appears below about pH 1 where water activity in the gel layer falls.`},{id:`q-pa-electro-16`,topicId:`pa-electro`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A small amount of gelatin or Triton X-100 is added to a polarographic solution in order to:`,options:[`Suppress the polarographic maximum that distorts the wave`,`Eliminate the migration current`,`Remove dissolved oxygen from the solution`,`Increase the drop time of the mercury capillary tenfold`],answer:0,explanation:`Streaming of solution over the mercury drop surface produces a spurious current peak at the top of the wave, and a trace of surface-active substance adsorbs on the drop and damps the streaming; too much of it, however, flattens the wave and depresses the diffusion current. Migration current is removed by an excess of indifferent supporting electrolyte, and dissolved oxygen is purged with nitrogen because it gives two reduction waves of its own.`}],n=[{id:`f-pa-errors-1`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Determinate error affects which quality: accuracy or precision?`,back:`Accuracy. Indeterminate (random) error affects precision.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-2`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Formula for sample standard deviation?`,back:`s = sqrt( sum(xi - xbar)^2 / (n - 1) )`},{id:`f-pa-errors-3`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`How many significant figures in 0.00250?`,back:`Three: 2, 5 and the trailing zero. Leading zeros never count.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-4`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`ICH Q2 formulae for LOD and LOQ from a calibration line?`,back:`LOD = 3.3 sigma / slope; LOQ = 10 sigma / slope.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-5`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`How do absolute uncertainties combine in a sum or difference?`,back:`In quadrature: s = sqrt(sa^2 + sb^2). Relative uncertainties combine that way for products and quotients.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-6`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Standard error of the mean?`,back:`SEM = s / sqrt(n)`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-1`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`Primary standard for standardising NaOH? And for HCl?`,back:`Potassium hydrogen phthalate (eq. wt. 204.2) for NaOH; anhydrous sodium carbonate (eq. wt. 53.0) for HCl.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-2`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`pH ranges of methyl orange and phenolphthalein?`,back:`Methyl orange 3.1 to 4.4 (red to yellow); phenolphthalein 8.3 to 10.0 (colourless to pink).`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-3`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`Difference between equivalence point and end point?`,back:`Equivalence point is the theoretical stoichiometric point; end point is the observed signal. The gap is the titration error.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-4`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`Indicator for weak acid vs strong base, and for strong acid vs weak base?`,back:`Phenolphthalein for weak acid; methyl orange (or methyl red) for weak base.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-5`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`What is the pH at half-neutralisation of a weak acid?`,back:`pH = pKa, because [salt] equals [acid].`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-6`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`Double indicator: what does P > M mean?`,back:`Mixture of sodium hydroxide and sodium carbonate. P < M means carbonate plus bicarbonate; P = M means pure carbonate.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-1`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Iodimetry versus iodometry in one line?`,back:`Iodimetry: standard iodine titrates a reducing agent directly. Iodometry: an oxidising agent liberates iodine from KI, which is titrated with thiosulphate.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-2`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Equivalent weight of KMnO4 in acid medium?`,back:`158 / 5 = 31.6 (five-electron change to Mn2+). In neutral or alkaline medium it is 158/3 = 52.7.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-3`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Equivalent weight of potassium dichromate as an oxidant?`,back:`294.2 / 6 = 49.03.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-4`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Why is only dilute sulphuric acid used in permanganometry?`,back:`HCl is oxidised to chlorine by an induced reaction and nitric acid is itself an oxidant; sulphuric acid is redox-inert.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-5`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Indicator for cerimetry and its colour change?`,back:`Ferroin, E0 = +1.06 V, red to pale blue at the end point.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-6`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Karl Fischer reagent composition and what it measures?`,back:`Iodine, sulphur dioxide, a base (pyridine or imidazole) and methanol. It measures water content.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-1`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`Denticity of EDTA and its metal:ligand ratio?`,back:`Hexadentate (2 N, 4 O donors); always 1:1 regardless of metal charge.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-2`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`Colour change with eriochrome black T at pH 10?`,back:`Wine red to blue at the end point.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-3`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`Indicators for Mohr, Volhard and Fajans methods?`,back:`Mohr: potassium chromate. Volhard: ferric ammonium sulphate. Fajans: adsorption dye such as fluorescein.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-4`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`Which argentometric method is a back titration and needs acidic medium?`,back:`Volhard, using excess silver nitrate then thiocyanate in nitric acid.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-5`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`Name two masking agents and what they mask.`,back:`Potassium cyanide masks Zn, Cd, Cu, Co, Ni, Hg; triethanolamine masks Al, Fe, Mn.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-6`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`How is calcium titrated in the presence of magnesium?`,back:`At pH 12 with murexide or calcon; magnesium precipitates as its hydroxide and does not react.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-1`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Standard system for titrating a weak base non-aqueously?`,back:`Glacial acetic acid as solvent, 0.1 M perchloric acid as titrant, crystal violet as indicator.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-2`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Why is acetic anhydride added to the perchloric acid titrant?`,back:`To remove the water in commercial 70 percent perchloric acid, which would otherwise level the acid.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-3`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Role of mercuric acetate in non-aqueous titration?`,back:`It replaces the interfering halide of a hydrochloride salt with weakly basic acetate, forming un-ionised mercuric halide.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-4`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Strongest acid species that can exist in glacial acetic acid?`,back:`The acetonium ion CH3COOH2+.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-5`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Solvent and titrant for weak acids such as barbiturates?`,back:`Dimethylformamide with 0.1 M sodium (or lithium) methoxide, thymol blue or azo violet indicator.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-6`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`What does potassium hydrogen phthalate act as in glacial acetic acid?`,back:`A base. It standardises perchloric acid there, though it acts as an acid in water.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-1`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`State the Beer-Lambert law in its pharmacopoeial form.`,back:`A = A(1 percent, 1 cm) x c x b, where c is in g per 100 mL and b in cm.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-2`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Convert specific absorbance to molar absorptivity.`,back:`epsilon = A(1 percent, 1 cm) x molecular weight / 10.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-3`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Define the four spectral shift terms.`,back:`Bathochromic = longer wavelength; hypsochromic = shorter; hyperchromic = more intense; hypochromic = less intense.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-4`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Sources and cell material for UV and for visible?`,back:`UV: deuterium lamp with quartz cells. Visible: tungsten lamp, glass cells acceptable.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-5`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Absorbance at which photometric error is minimum?`,back:`A = 0.4343, about 36.8 percent transmittance. Work between A 0.2 and 0.8.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-6`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Solvent polarity effect on n to pi* and pi to pi* bands?`,back:`n to pi* shifts hypsochromically (blue); pi to pi* shifts bathochromically (red).`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-1`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Selection rules for IR and for Raman?`,back:`IR needs a change in dipole moment; Raman needs a change in polarisability.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-2`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Wavenumber ranges: O-H, N-H, C-triple-bond-N, C=O, C=C?`,back:`O-H 3200-3600 broad, N-H 3300-3500, nitrile 2220-2260, C=O 1650-1820, alkene C=C 1620-1680.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-3`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Which region is the fingerprint region and why does it matter?`,back:`1500 to 400 cm-1. Skeletal and coupled vibrations make the pattern unique, so it identifies the exact compound.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-4`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Degrees of vibrational freedom for linear and non-linear molecules?`,back:`3N - 5 for linear, 3N - 6 for non-linear, where N is the number of atoms.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-5`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Name the three FTIR advantages.`,back:`Fellgett (multiplex), Jacquinot (throughput) and Connes (wavenumber accuracy).`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-6`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Order of C=O frequency: amide, ketone, ester, anhydride?`,back:`Anhydride 1800/1760 > ester 1740 > ketone 1715 > amide 1630-1690.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-1`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`Why is TMS the NMR reference?`,back:`Inert, volatile, soluble in organic solvents, twelve equivalent highly shielded protons giving one sharp peak at delta 0.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-2`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`State the n plus 1 rule and the intensity pattern.`,back:`n equivalent neighbouring protons split a signal into n+1 lines with Pascal triangle intensities (1:2:1 triplet, 1:3:3:1 quartet).`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-3`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`Chemical shift of an aldehyde proton and of a carboxylic acid proton?`,back:`CHO at delta 9.5 to 10.0; COOH at delta 10 to 13.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-4`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`State the nitrogen rule.`,back:`An even molecular ion mass means zero or an even number of nitrogens; an odd mass means an odd number of nitrogens.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-5`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`M : M+2 ratios for one Cl, one Br, two Cl?`,back:`One Cl = 3:1; one Br = 1:1; two Cl = 9:6:1.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-6`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`What does the McLafferty rearrangement require?`,back:`A gamma hydrogen and a carbonyl, transferred through a six-membered transition state with loss of a neutral alkene.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-1`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Formulae for N, HETP and Rf?`,back:`N = 16(tR/W)^2, HETP = L/N, Rf = solute distance / solvent front distance.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-2`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Capacity factor and resolution formulae?`,back:`k' = (tR - tM)/tM ; Rs = 2(tR2 - tR1)/(W1 + W2). Rs of 1.5 is baseline resolution.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-3`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Elution order in normal phase versus reversed phase?`,back:`Normal phase: non-polar elutes first. Reversed phase (C18): polar elutes first.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-4`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`What do the A, B and C terms of the van Deemter equation represent?`,back:`A = eddy diffusion, B = longitudinal diffusion, C = resistance to mass transfer.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-5`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`GC detectors: FID, TCD, ECD - what does each suit?`,back:`FID for organics (blind to water), TCD universal and non-destructive, ECD for halogenated and electronegative compounds.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-6`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Which chromatographic mode elutes the largest molecule first?`,back:`Size exclusion (gel filtration) - big molecules are excluded from the pores and travel the shortest path.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-1`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`Potentials of SCE and Ag/AgCl versus SHE?`,back:`SCE +0.244 V; silver-silver chloride +0.222 V; SHE is defined as 0.000 V.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-2`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`Nernst slope per decade at 25 degrees C?`,back:`59.2 mV for a monovalent ion, 29.6 mV for a divalent ion (0.0592/n).`},{id:`f-pa-electro-3`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`Write the Ilkovic equation.`,back:`id = 607 n D^(1/2) m^(2/3) t^(1/6) C, with id in microampere and C in millimole per litre.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-4`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`In polarography, which term is qualitative and which quantitative?`,back:`Half-wave potential E1/2 is qualitative; diffusion current id is quantitative.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-5`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`TGA, DTA and DSC - what does each measure?`,back:`TGA: mass change. DTA: temperature difference from a reference. DSC: heat flow, giving quantitative enthalpy.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-6`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`How does a glass transition appear on a DSC curve?`,back:`As a step (baseline shift from a change in heat capacity), not a peak.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-7`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Repeatability, intermediate precision, reproducibility - what changes in each?`,back:`Repeatability: same analyst, same instrument, short interval. Intermediate precision: different days, analysts or equipment in one laboratory. Reproducibility: different laboratories.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-8`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Dixon Q test formula and decision rule?`,back:`Q = |suspect - nearest neighbour| / range. Reject the suspect value only if calculated Q exceeds the tabulated Q.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-9`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Confidence interval of a mean?`,back:`CI = xbar +/- t s / sqrt(n), with t taken for (n - 1) degrees of freedom.`},{id:`f-pa-errors-10`,topicId:`pa-errors`,front:`Constant versus proportional determinate error?`,back:`Constant error is a fixed absolute amount, so its relative size falls as the sample is enlarged. Proportional error scales with sample size, so the percentage error is unchanged.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-7`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`pH at the equivalence point of a weak acid titrated with a strong base?`,back:`pH = 7 + 0.5 pKa + 0.5 log C, where C is the concentration of the salt formed.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-8`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`Why is mannitol added before titrating boric acid?`,back:`It chelates the borate to give a much stronger complex acid, which then gives a sharp phenolphthalein end point.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-9`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`Kjeldahl: what receives the distilled ammonia and which indicator is used?`,back:`An unmeasured excess of boric acid; the ammonium borate formed is titrated with standard acid using methyl red.`},{id:`f-pa-acidbase-10`,topicId:`pa-acidbase`,front:`How do you convert a titre into mass of analyte?`,back:`Milliequivalents = Normality x volume in mL; mass in mg = milliequivalents x equivalent weight.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-7`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Primary standards for permanganate, thiosulphate and iodine solutions?`,back:`Permanganate: sodium oxalate. Thiosulphate: potassium dichromate or potassium iodate via liberated iodine. Iodine: arsenic trioxide.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-8`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Volume strength of hydrogen peroxide from its normality?`,back:`Volume strength = normality x 5.6 (or molarity x 11.2).`},{id:`f-pa-redox-9`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`Why is phosphoric acid added in the dichromate titration of iron?`,back:`It complexes the ferric ion as colourless ferric phosphate, lowering the Fe3+/Fe2+ potential so the indicator changes sharply, and it also removes the yellow colour.`},{id:`f-pa-redox-10`,topicId:`pa-redox`,front:`What is the water equivalence factor of Karl Fischer reagent?`,back:`The mg of water consumed by 1 mL of reagent. It falls on storage, so it is redetermined with disodium tartrate dihydrate or standard water.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-7`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`Buffer and pH for the EDTA determination of total hardness?`,back:`Ammonia and ammonium chloride buffer at pH 10, with eriochrome black T.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-8`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`How is hardness converted to ppm as calcium carbonate?`,back:`mmol of EDTA x 100 mg gives mg of CaCO3; divide by the aliquot volume in litres to get mg per litre (ppm).`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-9`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`How does an adsorption indicator such as fluorescein signal the end point?`,back:`Past the equivalence point the precipitate surface becomes positively charged and adsorbs the dye anion, turning the solid pink.`},{id:`f-pa-complexo-precip-10`,topicId:`pa-complexo-precip`,front:`How stable must a metal-indicator complex be relative to the metal-EDTA complex?`,back:`Weaker, so EDTA can strip the metal at the end point. If it is stronger the indicator is blocked.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-7`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Name the four solvent classes with one example each.`,back:`Protogenic: glacial acetic acid. Protophilic: dimethylformamide. Amphiprotic: ethanol. Aprotic: benzene or chloroform.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-8`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Levelling versus differentiating solvent?`,back:`A levelling solvent makes all strong acids equal in strength (water). A differentiating solvent keeps them distinguishable (glacial acetic acid).`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-9`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`Two corrections applied to a non-aqueous titre?`,back:`A blank titration for basic impurities and moisture in the solvent, and a temperature correction of about 0.0011 per degree for glacial acetic acid.`},{id:`f-pa-nonaqueous-10`,topicId:`pa-nonaqueous`,front:`How much of a monoacidic base does 1 mL of 0.1 M perchloric acid neutralise?`,back:`0.1 mmol, that is molecular weight divided by 10 in mg. Halve it for a diacidic base.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-7`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Energy order of the four electronic transitions?`,back:`sigma to sigma* > n to sigma* > pi to pi* > n to pi*. Only the last two are seen above 200 nm.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-8`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`What is the isoabsorptive (isosbestic) point?`,back:`The wavelength at which two components have equal absorptivity, so absorbance depends only on total concentration.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-9`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Convert absorbance to percentage transmittance.`,back:`percent T = antilog(2 - A). A of 0.3 is about 50 percent T; A of 1.0 is 10 percent T.`},{id:`f-pa-uvvis-10`,topicId:`pa-uvvis`,front:`Typical epsilon values for allowed and for forbidden transitions?`,back:`Allowed pi to pi*: 10^3 to 10^5. Forbidden n to pi*: about 10 to 100.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-7`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Effect of conjugation and of ring strain on the C=O frequency?`,back:`Conjugation lowers it by 20 to 40 cm-1 (partial single bond character). Ring strain raises it: cyclopentanone 1745, cyclobutanone 1780 cm-1.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-8`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Three solid sampling techniques in IR, one line each.`,back:`KBr disc: sample ground with dry KBr and pressed. Nujol mull: paraffin, but hides C-H bands. ATR: evanescent wave from a ZnSe or diamond crystal, no preparation needed.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-9`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`How do you tell a carboxylic acid from a secondary amide in IR?`,back:`Acid: very broad O-H 3300 to 2500 over the C-H bands plus C=O at 1710. Amide: one sharp N-H at 3300, amide I 1660 and amide II 1550 cm-1.`},{id:`f-pa-ir-raman-10`,topicId:`pa-ir-raman`,front:`Which bond stretches at the higher wavenumber, C-H or C-D, and why?`,back:`C-H at about 3000; C-D near 2200. Wavenumber varies as 1/sqrt(reduced mass) and deuterium is heavier.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-7`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`Why deuterated solvents in proton NMR?`,back:`They contribute almost no proton signal and supply the deuterium lock. Residual CHCl3 still shows a small peak at delta 7.26.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-8`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`Signature pattern of an isopropyl group?`,back:`A 6H doublet for the two methyls and a 1H septet for the methine.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-9`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`How do you count carbons from the M+1 peak?`,back:`Number of carbons = (percent intensity of M+1 relative to M) divided by 1.1, since carbon-13 is 1.1 percent abundant.`},{id:`f-pa-nmr-ms-10`,topicId:`pa-nmr-ms`,front:`Does a coupling constant change with magnet strength? Does chemical shift in Hz?`,back:`J is fixed in Hz on any magnet. The separation of two shifts in Hz doubles when the field doubles, though delta in ppm is unchanged.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-7`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Plate count from the width at half height?`,back:`N = 5.54 (tR / W1/2)^2. Use 16 only with the baseline width taken from the tangents.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-8`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Doubling the column length does what to N, tR and Rs?`,back:`N doubles, tR and back pressure double, but Rs rises only by sqrt(2) = 1.41.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-9`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`Cause and cure of peak tailing for a basic drug on C18?`,back:`Residual free silanols acting as ion-exchange sites. Cure: end-capped column, pH about 3, or triethylamine as a competing base.`},{id:`f-pa-chromatography-10`,topicId:`pa-chromatography`,front:`What are ghost peaks in a blank gradient run?`,back:`Impurities from the mobile phase, water or previous injections that concentrate on the column head and elute as the gradient strengthens.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-7`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`How is the cell constant of a conductivity cell found?`,back:`Cell constant = specific conductance of standard KCl x measured resistance. Units: per cm.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-8`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`Equivalent conductance from specific conductance?`,back:`Equivalent conductance = 1000 k / C, with C in equivalents per litre. It rises on dilution while k falls.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-9`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`What is the alkaline error of a glass electrode?`,back:`Above about pH 9 the membrane also responds to sodium, so the reading is lower than the true pH. Lithium glass delays it to about pH 12.`},{id:`f-pa-electro-10`,topicId:`pa-electro`,front:`Why is gelatin or Triton X-100 added in polarography?`,back:`As a maximum suppressor, damping the streaming that causes the polarographic maximum. Too much depresses the diffusion current.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};